window.QUESTION_BANK = [{"module": 1, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A person purchases a condominium, lives in one room, and rents out the remaining rooms for income. Which statement best describes the property?</p>", "options": [["A", "It cannot be an investment because the owner lives there."], ["B", "It is necessarily a security rather than property."], ["C", "It can serve both personal consumption and investment purposes."], ["D", "It qualifies as an investment only if its market price increases."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An investment is defined by the expectation of income and/or appreciation. The condominium can supply housing services and rental income simultaneously. Appreciation is not required for an asset to qualify as an investment.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not assume that consumption and investment purposes are mutually exclusive.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 2, "question": "<p>An investor directly purchases a newly issued 10-year corporate bond. How should the investment be classified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Indirect, equity and short-term"], ["B", "Direct, debt and long-term"], ["C", "Direct, derivative and short-term"], ["D", "Indirect, debt and short-term"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor directly holds the security, acts as a creditor rather than an owner, and holds an instrument with a maturity longer than one year.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Investment classifications operate across several dimensions simultaneously.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 3, "question": "<p>An investor buys shares in a professionally managed mutual fund that owns hundreds of stocks. What is the most accurate description?</p>", "options": [["A", "An indirect investment that provides equity exposure"], ["B", "A direct ownership claim against every underlying company"], ["C", "A risk-free investment because it owns many securities"], ["D", "A fixed-income instrument because it distributes returns"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor owns an interest in the pooled fund rather than directly holding each underlying security. Diversification can reduce certain risks, but does not guarantee safety.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Owning units in a fund does not mean directly holding its underlying stocks.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Investment X has an expected return of 15%, while Investment Y has an expected return of 7%. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "X is superior because 15% is greater than 7%."], ["B", "Y is superior because low-return investments always have low risk."], ["C", "Neither can be judged without understanding risk and investor objectives."], ["D", "Both investments must have identical risk because returns are uncertain."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Expected return alone does not establish suitability. The investor must compare compensation with the uncertainty accepted and their investment goals, time horizon and liquidity needs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Expected return is not guaranteed return, and higher return is not automatically better.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 5, "question": "<p>An investor purchases an option whose value changes with the price of a company's shares. Why is the option classified as a derivative?</p>", "options": [["A", "It guarantees a fixed return."], ["B", "It grants ongoing ownership of the company."], ["C", "It requires the investor to lend money to the issuer."], ["D", "Its value is derived from an underlying asset."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The defining feature of a derivative is that its value is linked to an underlying asset. Debt and equity represent different economic claims.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An option does not automatically make its holder an ordinary shareholder.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 6, "question": "<p>According to the chapter's general classification, which group is typically a net supplier of funds?</p>", "options": [["A", "Individuals"], ["B", "Businesses expanding production"], ["C", "Governments financing projects"], ["D", "Corporations issuing securities to raise capital"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter generally classifies individuals as net suppliers of funds, while businesses and governments are typically net demanders. Individuals can still borrow for housing or cars.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Net supplier describes the group's typical aggregate role, not every individual transaction.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 7, "question": "<p>A pension fund manages the retirement savings of employees and buys securities on their behalf. It is best described as:</p>", "options": [["A", "An individual investor because the beneficiaries are people"], ["B", "An institutional investor because it manages funds for others"], ["C", "A direct investment because it invests in equities"], ["D", "A government investor regardless of who manages the fund"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Institutional investors manage money for other parties and commonly invest at scale. The identity of ultimate beneficiaries does not convert a pension fund into an individual investor.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The distinction is primarily the investor's role and structure, not the personal identity of beneficiaries.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 8, "question": "<p>A bank accepts deposits from households and channels funds to a manufacturing company through loans. What economic function is illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Conversion of all debt into equity"], ["B", "Elimination of all borrower default risk"], ["C", "Financial intermediation between suppliers and demanders of funds"], ["D", "A transaction that makes households net demanders of funds"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Financial intermediaries channel savings from those with available capital toward borrowers requiring funds. Intermediation does not eliminate credit risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Follow the direction of capital through the financial system.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Which feature most clearly distinguishes an exchange-traded fund (ETF) from a conventional mutual fund in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "An ETF must hold only bonds."], ["B", "An ETF cannot provide diversification."], ["C", "A conventional mutual fund must always be actively managed."], ["D", "ETF shares trade on exchanges during market trading hours."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both vehicles can pool investors' money and provide diversified exposure. The textbook emphasizes that ETFs can be bought and sold on exchanges while trading is open.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> ETFs are not defined by always being safer, riskier or passively managed.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Which description best matches hedge funds in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Government-guaranteed funds with no minimum subscription"], ["B", "Pooled investment vehicles that typically have higher minimums and lighter regulation than mutual funds"], ["C", "Funds that may invest only in government securities"], ["D", "Funds whose return must equal an index"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes hedge funds as pooling investors' resources, typically with larger minimum investments and less regulation than mutual funds, in its textbook context.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A hedge fund is not automatically a low-risk hedge against losses.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 11, "question": "<p>A person has RM20,000 in savings, unstable income, no emergency reserve and no adequate protection against major financial shocks. What should they prioritize first?</p>", "options": [["A", "Meeting investment prerequisites"], ["B", "Purchasing aggressive growth securities"], ["C", "Increasing leverage to build wealth faster"], ["D", "Selecting the investment with the highest historical return"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Step 1 of the seven-step investment process requires meeting basic needs, emergency cash requirements and protection against common financial risks before aggressive investing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The prerequisites precede portfolio construction for an economic reason, not bureaucratic formality.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 12, "question": "<p>An investor has identified retirement as a goal. They now create a written plan specifying a 25-year horizon, contribution targets and risk tolerance. Which investment step is primarily involved?</p>", "options": [["A", "Step 1: Meeting prerequisites"], ["B", "Step 2: Establishing goals"], ["C", "Step 3: Adopting an investment plan"], ["D", "Step 7: Managing the portfolio"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Establishing retirement as an objective is Step 2. Translating that objective into a written strategy with target dates and risk tolerance is Step 3.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish identifying an objective from designing a plan to achieve it.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A portfolio was designed for a 20-year retirement horizon. Five years later, the investor must access half the capital within 12 months. What is the most appropriate action?</p>", "options": [["A", "Keep the portfolio unchanged because the original plan was rational."], ["B", "Automatically sell every investment at once."], ["C", "Increase risk to make up for the shortened horizon."], ["D", "Reassess investment suitability and adjust the portfolio for the changed liquidity need."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor's circumstances have changed materially. Portfolio management requires comparing current conditions with expectations and taking suitable corrective action rather than mechanically maintaining an outdated allocation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A good investment plan is monitored and updated when relevant constraints change.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 14, "question": "<p>An investor purchases 20 securities from different companies. They claim the portfolio is therefore guaranteed not to suffer major losses. Which response is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because owning more than ten securities eliminates all risk."], ["B", "Incorrect, because diversification can reduce risk but does not guarantee against losses."], ["C", "Correct, provided the securities all belong to the same industry."], ["D", "Incorrect, because diversification cannot affect investment risk in any way."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter encourages diversified portfolios to manage risk, but it does not promise that diversification eliminates every risk or prevents broad-market losses.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Diversification is risk management, not a financial guarantee.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Before choosing a particular company's shares, an investor compares the expected returns and risks of several investment categories. Which step is most directly represented?</p>", "options": [["A", "Step 4: Evaluating investments"], ["B", "Step 5: Selecting suitable investments"], ["C", "Step 6: Constructing a diversified portfolio"], ["D", "Step 7: Managing the portfolio"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Step 4 involves evaluating potential return and risk. Step 5 moves toward researching and selecting specific investments, while Step 6 combines them into a portfolio.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Evaluation, product selection and portfolio construction are connected but distinct steps.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Assume Investment A earns 9% before tax and its entire return is taxed at 40%. Investment B earns 6% and is entirely tax-exempt. With otherwise comparable characteristics, which provides the higher after-tax return?</p>", "options": [["A", "A, because 9% exceeds 6%"], ["B", "Both provide 6%"], ["C", "They cannot be compared even with the tax assumptions given"], ["D", "B, because its after-tax return exceeds A's 5.4%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A returns 9% x (1 - 0.40) = 5.4% after tax, whereas B retains 6%. This illustrates the chapter's principle that investors should evaluate what they keep. The tax treatments here are hypothetical.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always apply the assumed taxes before comparing net returns.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Under the chapter's U.S. textbook classification of individual income, rental income from an investment property is generally an example of:</p>", "options": [["A", "Active income"], ["B", "Portfolio dividend income"], ["C", "Passive income"], ["D", "Interest income on debt"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides distinguish active income (wages and similar employment-related income), portfolio income (investment interest, dividends and capital gains), and passive income (including rents and royalties).</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the income classification given in the slides, rather than assuming all investment income belongs to the portfolio-income category.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A retiree relies on invested wealth to supplement living expenses and has limited capacity to recover from large losses. Which portfolio objective best fits the chapter's lifecycle framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Maximum speculation through derivatives"], ["B", "Preservation of capital and reliable investment income"], ["C", "Maximizing short-term share-price appreciation regardless of volatility"], ["D", "Concentrating all assets in one aggressive growth stock"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The retirement stage emphasizes capital preservation and income. The recommendation reflects the investor's cash-flow needs and reduced flexibility to recover from adverse outcomes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Match the asset characteristics to the investor's circumstances, not just the advertised return.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A 23-year-old student needs their savings to pay tuition fees in eight months. A friend recommends an aggressive equity strategy solely because the student is young. What is the strongest objection?</p>", "options": [["A", "The short investment horizon and need for capital preservation override an age-only recommendation."], ["B", "Every young person should invest entirely in bonds."], ["C", "Equities are never appropriate for investors below 30."], ["D", "Risk tolerance and liquidity requirements cannot affect investment decisions."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Although younger investors may generally have longer investing horizons, this specific money is needed soon. A short-term objective demands different asset characteristics from long-term retirement investing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Age is a useful lifecycle indicator, not an overriding decision rule.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 20, "question": "<p>What is the central purpose of the tax-advantaged retirement plans discussed in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "To guarantee high investment returns"], ["B", "To eliminate all investment risk"], ["C", "To ensure every participant can withdraw funds without restrictions"], ["D", "To provide tax advantages, including tax deferral for qualifying arrangements"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter discusses U.S. tax-advantaged retirement arrangements such as employer plans and IRAs. Their function is related to tax treatment and retirement saving, not investment guarantees.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Tax treatment, risk and investment return are different concepts. Not every plan has identical tax mechanics.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 21, "question": "<p>An investor needs RM50,000 for a house deposit in nine months and cannot tolerate a large temporary loss. Which approach best matches the objective?</p>", "options": [["A", "Concentrate the funds in a speculative growth stock."], ["B", "Use a liquid, relatively low-risk short-term instrument."], ["C", "Purchase highly volatile long-term securities and rely on appreciation."], ["D", "Use margin borrowing to increase the deposit amount."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The short horizon and need for reliable access make liquidity and capital stability especially important. High volatility can threaten the planned purchase even when a security is easy to sell.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Operational liquidity and stability of market value are not identical.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 22, "question": "<p>A property is worth approximately RM1 million under normal conditions, but the owner can sell immediately only by accepting RM750,000. Which characteristic is being compromised?</p>", "options": [["A", "Diversification"], ["B", "Expected dividend yield"], ["C", "Liquidity"], ["D", "Direct ownership"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Liquidity involves converting an investment into cash quickly with little or no loss in value. A large forced-sale discount shows that the property is not highly liquid under those circumstances.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An asset can be valuable without being easy to liquidate at a fair price.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A short-term deposit earns 3% annually, while inflation is 5%. Which statement best describes the result?</p>", "options": [["A", "The deposit is guaranteed to produce a 5% real return."], ["B", "It has become a derivative security."], ["C", "Inflation is irrelevant because the deposit remains liquid."], ["D", "Its nominal value may rise while purchasing power declines."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The account may increase in ringgit while inflation erodes the purchasing power of those ringgit. This is one of the disadvantages of low-return short-term investments highlighted in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Nominal principal protection is not the same as preservation of purchasing power.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Market interest rates rise substantially. All else equal, what would normally happen to the market price of an existing fixed-rate bond?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its market price would tend to decline."], ["B", "Its coupon rate would automatically increase to match market rates."], ["C", "Its price must rise because investors now prefer fixed-income securities."], ["D", "Its principal would automatically be converted into equity."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Newly issued securities can offer more attractive yields when rates rise. Existing bonds with fixed cash flows generally become less valuable at their previous prices, producing an inverse price-rate relationship.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish the fixed contractual coupon from the bond's changing market price.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Which instrument is most accurately described in the chapter as a short-term debt security issued by the U.S. Treasury, typically sold at a discount?</p>", "options": [["A", "Corporate common stock"], ["B", "Treasury bill"], ["C", "Long-term corporate bond"], ["D", "Equity mutual fund"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The short-term investment comparison identifies Treasury bills as U.S. Treasury instruments with maturities of one year or less and normally sold at a discount.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse Treasury bills with long-term government debt instruments.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 26, "question": "<p>A large, creditworthy corporation wants to obtain financing for approximately 90 days by issuing an unsecured short-term note. Which instrument best fits the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Preferred stock"], ["B", "Commercial paper"], ["C", "Common stock"], ["D", "A long-term convertible bond"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Commercial paper is described as an unsecured short-term note issued by a company with high credit standing. Preferred and common stock are equity-type claims, not short-term unsecured notes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Identify the issuer, contractual nature and maturity of the financing.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investor keeps a portion of their portfolio in short-term instruments while waiting for a more attractive long-term opportunity. Which interpretation is most consistent with the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "The investor has abandoned investing because short-term holdings are not investments."], ["B", "The investor must be making a mistake because all capital should always be fully invested in equities."], ["C", "The holdings are unsuitable unless their yield exceeds all stock returns."], ["D", "The investor is using short-term instruments as a temporary outlet that maintains flexibility."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter specifically recognizes short-term investments as temporary places for funds while investors wait for attractive longer-term opportunities. Liquidity itself can have economic value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A lower expected return may be a rational price for maintaining flexibility.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 28, "question": "<p>GDP and industrial production are expanding, and unemployment is falling. What is the most appropriate conclusion about stock prices under the chapter's business-cycle discussion?</p>", "options": [["A", "The environment is generally associated with stronger markets, but does not guarantee gains for every stock."], ["B", "Every stock must rise because the economy is expanding."], ["C", "Stock prices are mathematically independent of business conditions."], ["D", "The economy's expansion guarantees that bonds and shares earn equal returns."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes that share prices tend to rise during expansions and weaken during declines. This is a broad tendency, not a deterministic relationship for individual securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish an economic association from a guaranteed investment outcome.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A manufacturing company wants assistance raising capital by issuing securities to investors. Which finance career is most directly associated with that work?</p>", "options": [["A", "Personal insurance claims management"], ["B", "Commercial bank deposit administration"], ["C", "Investment banking"], ["D", "Household retirement budgeting"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies investment banking as a field that helps organizations raise capital. Other finance professions may be involved in planning, insurance or routine banking activities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Investment banking and investment management are not the same career function.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 30, "question": "<p>An investor has two goals: RM50,000 for a house in one year and retirement savings for 30 years. What is the strongest overall investment strategy?</p>", "options": [["A", "Separate the goals into suitable allocations: liquid, low-risk assets for the house and longer-horizon investments for retirement."], ["B", "Combine everything into one aggressive growth portfolio because retirement is the longer goal."], ["C", "Invest everything in cash forever because the house purchase is near."], ["D", "Choose the asset with the highest historical return and apply it to both goals."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The two goals have different time horizons, liquidity requirements and capacities for loss. A goal-based strategy can use short-term instruments for the house deposit while taking suitable longer-term growth exposure for retirement.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> One investor can have multiple goals requiring different investment strategies.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A corporation issues a new 10-year bond to raise money for factory expansion. An investor purchases the bond directly from the new offering. Which classification is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Primary money market"], ["B", "Secondary capital market"], ["C", "Primary capital market"], ["D", "Secondary money market"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bond is newly issued, so the transaction is in the primary market. Its 10-year maturity makes it a capital-market security. Primary/secondary and money/capital are separate classification dimensions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always classify the issuance status and maturity separately.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Investor A sells 1,000 existing shares of ABC Berhad to Investor B through a stock exchange. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "ABC Berhad receives the sales proceeds as new capital."], ["B", "Investor A receives the proceeds; the transaction occurs in the secondary market."], ["C", "The transaction must be classified as a primary-market rights offering."], ["D", "Investor B becomes a lender rather than a shareholder."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In a normal secondary-market resale, the seller receives the proceeds. The company does not directly raise new capital from that resale, even though secondary-market trading supports liquidity and price discovery.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Follow the money: who actually receives the transaction proceeds?</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 3, "question": "<p>A government issues a new Treasury bill maturing in 90 days. An investor buys it at issuance. Which market classification is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Primary money market"], ["B", "Secondary capital market"], ["C", "Primary capital market"], ["D", "Secondary money market"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A newly issued instrument belongs to the primary market, and a 90-day maturity places the Treasury bill in the money market.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A government security is not automatically a long-term capital-market instrument.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 4, "question": "<p>A student claims secondary markets are economically unimportant because companies receive no new funds when previously issued shares are resold. What is the strongest response?</p>", "options": [["A", "The student is correct because resale trading serves no economic function."], ["B", "Secondary markets create new company shares every time an investor buys."], ["C", "Secondary markets guarantee the price of newly issued shares."], ["D", "Secondary markets improve liquidity and price discovery, making initial investment more attractive."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides identify liquidity and continuous pricing as central secondary-market functions. Investors may be more willing to buy newly issued securities when they expect to have a practical way to sell later.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A market can add economic value without directly transferring fresh capital to the issuer.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 5, "question": "<p>What distinguishes an initial public offering (IPO) from an ordinary secondary-market purchase of shares?</p>", "options": [["A", "An IPO is the first public sale of a company's stock."], ["B", "An IPO occurs only when existing investors trade among themselves."], ["C", "An IPO always sells corporate bonds rather than shares."], ["D", "An IPO guarantees positive capital gains to subscribing investors."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines an IPO as the first public sale of company stock. A secondary-market purchase involves securities that have already been issued.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Going public is not the same as routine trading after the company is public.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 6, "question": "<p>A listed corporation gives its existing shareholders the opportunity to subscribe for additional newly issued shares in proportion to their existing holdings. Which primary-market method best describes this?</p>", "options": [["A", "Private placement"], ["B", "Secondary-market resale"], ["C", "Rights offering"], ["D", "Short sale"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies rights offerings as a primary-market distribution alternative. They provide existing shareholders with subscription rights to purchase newly issued shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Issuing additional shares to existing owners is not simply a secondary-market transaction.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 7, "question": "<p>A company sells a new securities issue privately to a small group of selected institutional investors rather than broadly offering it to the public. Which method best describes the transaction?</p>", "options": [["A", "Rights offering"], ["B", "Private placement"], ["C", "Fourth-market resale"], ["D", "Ordinary secondary-market exchange trade"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter lists private placement as a primary-market issuance method. Here the new issue is offered to a selected group instead of being broadly distributed through a public offering.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Private placement concerns how newly issued securities are distributed; it is not synonymous with secondary trading.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 8, "question": "<p>During an IPO waiting period, prospective investors receive a preliminary offering document containing information about the company and proposed issue. What is this document called?</p>", "options": [["A", "Tombstone"], ["B", "Final audit report"], ["C", "Selling-group agreement"], ["D", "Red herring"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides define a red herring as the preliminary prospectus available during the waiting period. The final prospectus is the broader formal disclosure document describing the issue and issuer.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse the preliminary prospectus with the roadshow, tombstone or final prospectus.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 9, "question": "<p>An investment bank purchases a securities issue from the company at an agreed price and assumes the risk of reselling it to investors. What is the bank primarily doing?</p>", "options": [["A", "Acting as a guaranteed investment-return provider"], ["B", "Operating as a short seller of the issuing company's shares"], ["C", "Underwriting the offering and accepting distribution risk"], ["D", "Eliminating all risks associated with the issuing company"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Under the chapter's described underwriting arrangement, the investment bank purchases the issue and bears the financial risk of reselling it. This does not eliminate the issuer's business risk or guarantee returns to public investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish underwriting distribution risk from the investment risk borne by shareholders.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 10, "question": "<p>A large IPO involves several investment banks sharing underwriting risk, while other brokerage firms help sell the shares to investors. Which distinction is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The underwriting syndicate shares financial risk; the selling group helps distribute securities."], ["B", "The selling group guarantees all future investor returns."], ["C", "The underwriting syndicate exists solely to regulate the company's accounting."], ["D", "The selling group and underwriting syndicate have no different responsibilities."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter distinguishes the underwriting syndicate, which shares underwriting risk, from the selling group, which assists in marketing and distributing the securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk sharing and distribution assistance are related functions, but not the same responsibility.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 11, "question": "<p>In Transaction A, an intermediary matches a buyer with a seller. In Transaction B, an intermediary purchases securities from a seller and later resells them from its own inventory. What are the roles?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is dealer; B is broker"], ["B", "Both are brokers"], ["C", "Both are dealers"], ["D", "A is broker; B is dealer"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A broker primarily facilitates trades between investors. A dealer trades as principal and acts as a counterparty. The dealer may bear inventory and market-price risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The central distinction is whether the intermediary merely matches the trade or becomes a trading counterparty.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 12, "question": "<p>A dealer quotes a bid of RM50.80 and an ask of RM51.20. You want to immediately sell 100 shares to the dealer. What is the gross amount received?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM5,120"], ["B", "RM5,080"], ["C", "RM5,100"], ["D", "RM40"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bid is the price the dealer offers to pay when buying. Selling 100 shares at RM50.80 gives 100 x RM50.80 = RM5,080, before transaction costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Bid and ask quotations are defined from the market maker's perspective.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A dealer quotes RM40.00 bid and RM40.40 ask. Ignoring other costs and price changes, an investor buys 100 shares from the dealer and immediately sells them back. What is the investor's loss?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM0"], ["B", "RM20"], ["C", "RM40"], ["D", "RM80"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor buys at the ask for RM4,040 and sells at the bid for RM4,000. The RM40 difference reflects the bid-ask spread for 100 shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Even without a change in quoted market prices, crossing the spread creates a transaction cost.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 14, "question": "<p>Two pension funds execute a large securities transaction directly with each other through an electronic communications network, bypassing a market maker. Which market structure best matches the textbook?</p>", "options": [["A", "Fourth market"], ["B", "Third market"], ["C", "Primary market"], ["D", "Traditional dealer market"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The fourth market involves institutional investors transacting directly with one another, often using ECNs to bypass market makers. The third market involves institutional trading through non-exchange-member market makers.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Third market uses a market maker; fourth market bypasses one.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Under the chapter's historical description, an investor is considering a small, thinly traded company quoted on the OTC Pink Sheets. Which concern is most consistent with the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "The company must satisfy the strictest exchange-listing rules."], ["B", "The security is guaranteed to be as liquid as a large NYSE stock."], ["C", "The security cannot experience a capital loss."], ["D", "Liquidity may be extremely low and the investment may be highly risky."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes Pink Sheets securities as small, frequently illiquid and potentially highly speculative. Its regulatory descriptions are historical and should not be treated as a current universal legal rule.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Trading access does not imply liquidity, listing quality or investor protection.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Which combination most closely describes a bull market in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Falling prices, pessimism and economic slowdown"], ["B", "Rising prices, optimism and favorable market conditions"], ["C", "Flat prices, zero volatility and guaranteed dividends"], ["D", "Rising bond coupons and falling economic activity"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The textbook associates bull markets with rising security prices, optimism and generally favorable market conditions, while bear markets involve falling prices and pessimism.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Market sentiment and broad direction are not guarantees about every security.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Why can an internationally diversified portfolio have lower overall risk than a portfolio invested entirely in one domestic market?</p>", "options": [["A", "International securities always have higher returns."], ["B", "Foreign currencies eliminate financial risk."], ["C", "Different markets may move imperfectly together, reducing portfolio concentration."], ["D", "Foreign governments guarantee investors against losses."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter explains that international markets do not necessarily move in the same direction or at the same time. Different economic cycles can provide diversification benefits, although new risks are introduced.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Diversification benefit depends on return relationships, not merely owning foreign securities.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A Malaysian investor buys a U.S. stock. Its USD value rises 10%, but the USD depreciates 12% against MYR during the holding period. Ignoring distributions and costs, what is the MYR return?</p>", "options": [["A", "+22%"], ["B", "-2%"], ["C", "+3.2%"], ["D", "-3.2%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The correct currency-adjusted return is (1.10 x 0.88) - 1 = -3.2%. A gain in the asset's foreign currency can be more than offset by depreciation of that currency against the investor's home currency.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not simply add a stock return and an exchange-rate movement when calculating the exact combined return.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A U.S. investor wants exposure to a foreign company's shares through a U.S.-traded depositary instrument. Which instrument best matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "American Depositary Receipt (ADR)"], ["B", "U.S. Treasury bill"], ["C", "Corporate commercial paper"], ["D", "A domestic government savings account"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies ADRs as dollar-denominated receipts representing shares of foreign companies held by a depositary bank, providing a route to foreign-equity exposure.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Trading or being denominated in USD does not eliminate exposure to the underlying foreign investment.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A foreign government unexpectedly changes securities taxation and capital-transfer restrictions, impairing overseas investors' ability to recover proceeds. Which risk category is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only ordinary stock-price volatility"], ["B", "Government-policy and political/regulatory risk"], ["C", "Only broker inventory risk"], ["D", "No investment risk because the assets remain foreign"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies government-policy risk from foreign laws, regulation and political conditions. Currency risk can also affect international investing, but the scenario principally concerns legal and policy changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Differentiate changes in exchange rates from changes in government rules.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 21, "question": "<p>A company preparing a U.S. public securities issue is subject to a law emphasizing disclosure about the issuer and offering. Which textbook law is the best match?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002"], ["B", "Maloney Act of 1938"], ["C", "Securities Act of 1933"], ["D", "Insider Trading and Fraud Act of 1988"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter associates the Securities Act of 1933 with securities-offering disclosure. Sarbanes-Oxley emphasizes accounting and audit controls, while the other laws address different regulatory issues.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The 1933 Act focuses on disclosure; the 1934 Act established the SEC in the chapter.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes the Securities Act of 1934 from the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 in the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "The 1934 Act established the SEC; Sarbanes-Oxley strengthened accounting and audit requirements."], ["B", "Both laws were introduced specifically to govern short-selling margins."], ["C", "The 1934 Act created all mutual funds; Sarbanes-Oxley established the Nasdaq market."], ["D", "Sarbanes-Oxley eliminated the need for financial reporting."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>According to the chapter, the 1934 Act established the SEC as the federal regulator, while Sarbanes-Oxley tightened accounting and audit guidelines to reduce corporate fraud.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Learn each regulation by the market failure or institutional function it addresses.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A corporate executive trades shares using material nonpublic information about an impending earnings announcement. Which statement best matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "The activity is automatically permitted because the executive owns the shares."], ["B", "It is a form of ordinary diversification."], ["C", "The trade is legitimate as long as a broker places it."], ["D", "It raises insider-trading concerns because nonpublic company information is being used in securities transactions."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides identify insider trading as using nonpublic company information for profitable securities transactions. Ownership of the security or use of a broker does not itself justify use of privileged information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The problem concerns information advantages, not the mechanics of execution.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 24, "question": "<p>According to the regular U.S. trading session stated in the chapter, which time range applies to the major U.S. exchanges and Nasdaq?</p>", "options": [["A", "8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., Eastern"], ["B", "9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m., Eastern"], ["C", "4:00 p.m. to 6:30 p.m., Eastern"], ["D", "9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m., Malaysian time"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides specify regular U.S. trading hours of 9:30 a.m.-4:00 p.m. Eastern. They discuss extended-hour trading separately. The exact extended sessions in the older slides should not be assumed current.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish regular trading sessions from extended-hours sessions and keep the timezone explicit.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An investor purchases 100 shares at RM50 each and later sells them at RM60. The investor receives no dividends. Ignoring costs, which description is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A short sale producing RM1,000 profit"], ["B", "A margin trade with necessarily unlimited losses"], ["C", "A long purchase producing RM1,000 capital gain"], ["D", "A long purchase producing RM500 capital gain"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor buys first and later sells at a higher price, making this a long purchase. The capital gain is 100 x (RM60 - RM50) = RM1,000.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Identify the order of buying and selling before analyzing the profit.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 26, "question": "<p>An investor buys RM10,000 of securities subject to a hypothetical 50% initial margin requirement. How much personal equity and borrowed money are needed?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM5,000 equity and RM5,000 borrowing"], ["B", "RM10,000 equity and no borrowing"], ["C", "RM2,500 equity and RM7,500 borrowing"], ["D", "RM7,500 equity and RM2,500 borrowing"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A 50% initial margin means the investor contributes half the initial purchase value. Thus equity is RM5,000 and the broker loan is RM5,000.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Initial margin is the proportion funded by the investor, not the amount borrowed.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investor controls RM10,000 of stock using RM5,000 equity and RM5,000 borrowed money. The stock subsequently increases 20%, while the loan balance stays constant. What is the gain on the investor's initial equity, ignoring costs?</p>", "options": [["A", "+20%"], ["B", "+40%"], ["C", "+10%"], ["D", "+100%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The position gains RM2,000 in value. Relative to the investor's RM5,000 original equity, the gain is 2,000/5,000 = 40%. If the stock had fallen 20%, the equity loss would similarly be 40% before costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Leverage magnifies percentage outcomes measured against equity rather than total securities value.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A margin investor contributed RM2,500 equity to purchase RM5,000 of shares. They later sold the shares for RM7,500, received RM100 dividends and paid RM125 margin interest. What was the return on initial invested equity, ignoring other costs?</p>", "options": [["A", "95%"], ["B", "100%"], ["C", "120%"], ["D", "99%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Use the chapter's margin-return formula: [RM100 - RM125 + RM7,500 - RM5,000] / RM2,500 = RM2,475/RM2,500 = 99%. Financing costs reduce the return, even when the asset's price appreciation is substantial.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include both investment income and borrowing interest in the numerator, and use initial investor equity as denominator.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A margin account holds securities worth RM6,500 and has a debit balance of RM1,200. What is its current margin ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "18.5%"], ["B", "54.2%"], ["C", "81.5%"], ["D", "120.0%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Margin = (V - D)/V = (6,500 - 1,200)/6,500 = 5,300/6,500 ≈ 81.5%. This measures investor equity as a proportion of current securities value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not calculate borrowed funds divided by value and call that the investor's margin.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A short seller borrows and sells 100 shares at RM50 each. Later they repurchase the shares for RM30 and return them to the lender. Ignoring all costs, what is the profit?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM2,000 profit"], ["B", "RM2,000 loss"], ["C", "RM3,000 profit"], ["D", "RM5,000 profit"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Initial sale proceeds are RM5,000. Repurchasing 100 shares at RM30 costs RM3,000. Profit = RM5,000 - RM3,000 = RM2,000. A short seller profits when the share price falls.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Short selling reverses the usual trade sequence: borrow shares, sell, repurchase, then return the shares.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 31, "question": "<p>A short seller sells 100 borrowed shares at RM50 and deposits RM2,500 additional margin. The account holds RM7,500. The shares rise to RM70, and the broker requires a 30% maintenance margin. Which conclusion is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The margin is 50%, so no margin call is possible."], ["B", "The investor has RM2,500 equity because initial margin never changes."], ["C", "The investor profits because the share price has increased."], ["D", "The margin falls to about 7.1%, so a margin call is triggered under the stated requirement."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The liability to repurchase the shares is now RM7,000. Short-account equity = RM7,500 - RM7,000 = RM500. Margin = RM500/RM7,000 ≈ 7.14%, below the 30% requirement. Rising share prices hurt the short seller because the repurchase obligation becomes more expensive.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> For a short sale, the broker-held funds are offset by the changing cost of buying back the borrowed shares.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor argues that online trading platforms necessarily improve investment performance because they provide faster execution, more information and lower commissions. Which response is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because greater access to information guarantees profitable decisions."], ["B", "Incorrect, because online investing always produces lower returns than traditional investing."], ["C", "Online tools improve accessibility, but investment outcomes still depend on decision quality, costs and risk management."], ["D", "Correct, provided the investor trades frequently enough."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter recognizes the advantages of convenient research and execution but warns about unreliable advice, excessive trading, transaction costs, lack of a live broker as a safety net and margin risks. Faster trading is not the same as better investing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish technological efficiency from the quality of an investment decision.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 2, "question": "<p>A company's annual report shows that revenue increased by 12% and net income declined by 4% last year. How should this information primarily be classified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Analytical, because it predicts future stock prices"], ["B", "Descriptive, because it reports historical company performance"], ["C", "Speculative, because accounting statements cannot contain facts"], ["D", "Advisory, because every annual report contains a recommendation to purchase shares"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Descriptive information presents factual data concerning historical behavior. Revenue and earnings figures in a company report are historical observations, rather than forecasts or recommendations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Observed financial performance is not automatically a forecast of future performance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 3, "question": "<p>An analyst observes that Company X's revenue rose 15% last year and then predicts that its shares will appreciate 25% next year. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both statements are purely descriptive."], ["B", "The revenue figure is analytical and the price prediction is descriptive."], ["C", "Both statements are guaranteed facts because an analyst made them."], ["D", "The revenue figure is descriptive, while the stock-price forecast is analytical."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The historical revenue figure reports what happened. The share-price forecast uses assumptions and judgment to estimate what might happen. The chapter distinguishes descriptive data from analytical projections and recommendations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Professional forecasts remain forecasts, even if they are based on genuine financial data.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 4, "question": "<p>An investor wants to inspect a U.S. public company's annual financial disclosures rather than rely solely on social media summaries. Which source from the chapter is most directly relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "The company's Form 10-K filing"], ["B", "An anonymous investment discussion thread"], ["C", "A promotional newsletter with no disclosed sources"], ["D", "An unrelated market index price chart"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies Form 10-K as an annual filing submitted to the SEC by public companies in its U.S. context. Company filings offer direct information for verification and analysis.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Primary-source disclosure is more suitable for verifying reported financial results than unsupported commentary.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A publicly traded company discloses material corporate information to selected professional analysts before making it available to ordinary investors. Which regulatory principle discussed in Chapter 3 is most directly relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "The principle that every investor must earn the same return"], ["B", "The rule that all investments require identical brokerage commissions"], ["C", "Regulation FD's requirement concerning simultaneous public and professional disclosure"], ["D", "The rule that all analysts must own shares in companies they cover"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter discusses Regulation FD as requiring important company information to be disclosed simultaneously to investment professionals and the public. Its purpose relates to unequal access to material company information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The central issue is selective disclosure, not whether investors eventually earn identical returns.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 6, "question": "<p>An online promoter buys a small company's shares, publishes exaggerated claims to attract investors, and sells their holdings after the share price rises. What scheme best describes this?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ordinary portfolio rebalancing"], ["B", "Pump-and-dump"], ["C", "An investment club's research process"], ["D", "A legitimate capital raising exercise by the issuer"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A pump-and-dump scheme involves promoting or hyping a security to inflate market demand and price, then selling into the artificially created demand. The promoter's incentives may conflict with those of later investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Ask who benefits when investors act on the information being promoted.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 7, "question": "<p>An unlicensed promoter offers an investment promising 25% monthly returns with no possibility of loss. The investment structure is unclear. What is the most appropriate response using the chapter's investor-protection questions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Invest quickly because the potential return compensates for missing documentation."], ["B", "Ignore licensing because promised returns are more important."], ["C", "Assume the investment is legitimate if many people recommend it."], ["D", "Verify the seller, registration, risk-reward characteristics and investment mechanism before committing capital."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter recommends checking whether sellers are licensed, investments are registered, risks are understood and investor recourse exists. Unusually high guaranteed returns and opacity are reasons for greater scrutiny.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> High promised return is not proof of legitimacy or low risk.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Your equity portfolio earned 8% last year, while its appropriately matched market benchmark earned 20%. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio earned a positive absolute return but underperformed its benchmark by 12 percentage points."], ["B", "The portfolio outperformed because any positive return is automatically superior."], ["C", "The portfolio must have lower risk because its return was lower."], ["D", "The market benchmark is irrelevant whenever a portfolio is profitable."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An 8% return is positive in absolute terms but 12 percentage points below the relevant benchmark's 20% return. The comparison indicates relative underperformance, although a complete evaluation should also consider risk and the portfolio's objectives.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Separate absolute performance from relative performance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 9, "question": "<p>According to the chapter, what most clearly distinguishes a stock market index from a simple market average?</p>", "options": [["A", "An index includes only bonds, whereas an average includes equities."], ["B", "An index measures market behavior relative to a previously established base value."], ["C", "An index always guarantees a return matching inflation."], ["D", "Averages are always more diversified than indexes."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter distinguishes averages based on arithmetic price behavior from indexes reflecting values relative to a base period. Both help summarize market performance, but they use different measurement constructions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An index is a measurement of a specified securities basket, not a promise of investment performance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 10, "question": "<p>An investor wants a broader indicator of major U.S. equities instead of one limited to 30 selected large companies. Which index is more suitable under the chapter's comparison?</p>", "options": [["A", "Dow Jones Transportation Average"], ["B", "Dow Jones Utilities Average"], ["C", "S&amp;P 500 Composite Index"], ["D", "Dow Jones Industrial Average"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes the S&amp;P 500 as covering 500 companies across major sectors and as more representative of the broad stock market than the 30-stock DJIA.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The most familiar market average is not necessarily the broadest benchmark.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 11, "question": "<p>An investor wants an index in which smaller constituent companies are not overshadowed solely because other companies have larger market values. Which method best addresses that concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "Use a portfolio weighted entirely toward the largest company."], ["B", "Exclude every company with a market value below the average."], ["C", "Use an index consisting of just one large stock."], ["D", "Use equal weighting, as described for the Value Line Composite Index."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter highlights the Value Line Composite's equal-weighting approach as a method of reducing the domination of an index by large market-value companies. The weighting method affects how index returns are measured.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Two indexes holding similar securities can behave differently if their weighting rules differ.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 12, "question": "<p>An investor is studying U.S. small-company stocks and wants the most directly relevant benchmark among the indices discussed in the slides. Which one should they consider?</p>", "options": [["A", "Russell 2000 Index"], ["B", "Russell 1000 Index"], ["C", "Dow Jones Utilities Average"], ["D", "Dow Jones Transportation Average"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies the Russell 2000 as covering smaller companies and the Russell 1000 as covering larger companies. The benchmark should represent the segment being analyzed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always match the benchmark's investment universe to the portfolio being evaluated.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A bond pays a fixed coupon, but an investor purchases it below its face value and intends to hold it to maturity. Why might its yield differ from its coupon rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Coupon payments automatically change whenever the market price changes."], ["B", "The bond becomes ordinary equity when purchased below face value."], ["C", "Yield incorporates the return relative to the price paid, including the effect of maturity value."], ["D", "Yield must always equal the bond's original stated coupon rate."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's bond-yield discussion emphasizes total return associated with buying a bond at its current price and holding it to maturity. The price paid matters because the investor can receive a gain or loss relative to the bond's maturity repayment.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Coupon rate and yield are not interchangeable, especially when the bond trades away from face value.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 14, "question": "<p>An investor's shares are registered in the brokerage firm's name but held for the investor, who remains the beneficial owner. What arrangement is described?</p>", "options": [["A", "Short selling"], ["B", "Street-name registration"], ["C", "A custodial account that transfers ownership permanently"], ["D", "A corporate rights offering"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes street name as securities being registered in the brokerage firm's name while the client retains beneficial ownership. It is a custody and administration arrangement, not a sale of ownership to the broker.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Legal registration of securities and beneficial economic ownership need not be in the same name.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 15, "question": "<p>An experienced investor conducts independent research, makes their own investment decisions and requires only cost-efficient trade execution. Which broker type is most aligned with that need?</p>", "options": [["A", "A full-service broker charging more for extensive advisory services"], ["B", "A professional advisor with discretionary control regardless of fees"], ["C", "An investment club instead of a brokerage account"], ["D", "A basic discount broker focused on transaction execution"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes basic discount brokers as concentrating on electronic execution with little or no research or investment advice. For an investor who does not require extra advisory services, avoiding unnecessary fees is economically sensible.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Paying more for services is rational only when those services add sufficient value.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A commission-based broker repeatedly encourages a client to buy and sell securities even though the trades serve no clear investment purpose. What is the main concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "Churning to generate commissions"], ["B", "Improved diversification through every additional trade"], ["C", "Regulation FD disclosure violations by the issuing company"], ["D", "A guaranteed increase in after-tax returns"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter warns that churning involves excessive trading of client accounts to increase commissions. More trading can benefit an intermediary while hurting the client through costs and poor decisions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Consider whether the intermediary's compensation incentives align with the client's objectives.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 17, "question": "<p>An investor wishes to borrow part of the funds needed to purchase shares, using securities as collateral. Which brokerage account type is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A cash account, which automatically provides unsecured loans"], ["B", "A custodial account for a minor"], ["C", "A margin account with borrowing privileges"], ["D", "A wrap account, which guarantees leveraged gains"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies margin accounts as brokerage accounts that provide borrowing privileges. A cash account does not serve the same financing role; margin borrowing also introduces financing costs and magnified losses.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An account's purpose is determined by the rights and obligations it creates, not merely by which securities it can hold.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 18, "question": "<p>An investor prefers to delegate investment decisions to a professional manager and pay a flat annual account fee. Which account type in the chapter best fits this arrangement?</p>", "options": [["A", "Cash account"], ["B", "Wrap account"], ["C", "Odd-lot account"], ["D", "Self-directed margin account"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines a wrap account as shifting investment decisions to a professional money manager in return for an annual account fee. This differs from an execution-only arrangement.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Delegating investment decisions is different from merely receiving research or execution services.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A stock is trading actively. An investor must sell their position as quickly as reasonably possible and is willing to accept the best available execution price. Which order best matches the objective?</p>", "options": [["A", "A buy limit order"], ["B", "A good-'til-canceled buy order"], ["C", "A sell limit far above the current market"], ["D", "A market sell order"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A market order prioritizes immediate execution at the available market price. Unlike a limit order, it does not guarantee an exact selling price and can be affected by liquidity or price changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Execution priority and price protection are different objectives.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A stock is quoted around RM50. An investor wants to purchase it only if the execution price is RM47 or lower. What is the most suitable instruction?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy limit at RM47"], ["B", "Buy market order"], ["C", "Sell stop at RM47"], ["D", "Buy stop at RM53"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A buy limit order specifies the maximum acceptable price. The trade can execute at RM47 or below if a suitable seller is available, but execution is not guaranteed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A buy limit sets a ceiling on the price paid, not a guaranteed future purchase.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 21, "question": "<p>An investor owns shares trading near RM60 and will sell only for RM65 or more. Which instruction is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market sell immediately"], ["B", "Buy limit at RM65"], ["C", "Sell limit at RM65"], ["D", "Sell stop at RM65"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A sell limit establishes a minimum acceptable selling price. An order at RM65 can fill at RM65 or higher, subject to market conditions, but may remain unfilled.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Buy limits establish maximum purchase prices; sell limits establish minimum selling prices.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 22, "question": "<p>An investor submits a fill-or-kill order to purchase 500 shares at a specified limit price. Only 300 shares can be executed immediately under the order's conditions. What should happen?</p>", "options": [["A", "The broker must purchase the other 200 shares at any price."], ["B", "The order is canceled rather than partially filled."], ["C", "The remaining 200 shares must stay open for six months."], ["D", "The order automatically becomes a stop-loss order."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes fill-or-kill orders as requiring immediate execution or cancellation. The stated quantity cannot be completely filled, so the order is canceled rather than partially executed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse fill-or-kill with day orders or GTC instructions.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A stock trades at RM20. The investor places a sell stop at RM17. Overnight, adverse news causes the first available trading price to fall to RM14, triggering the order. What is the most accurate conclusion?</p>", "options": [["A", "The broker must sell at RM17 regardless of market conditions."], ["B", "The stop order cannot execute if the price falls below RM17."], ["C", "The stop becomes a limit order at RM17."], ["D", "The stop can become a market order and execute near RM14, rather than at the stop price."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter states that a stop order becomes a market order when activated. RM17 is the triggering level, not a guaranteed execution price. A gap down can produce substantial slippage.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Stop price is a trigger, not an insurance contract against losses beyond that level.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 24, "question": "<p>An investor places a sell stop-limit order with a stop trigger of RM18 and a minimum selling limit of RM17.50. The stock gaps down to RM15 without available bids at RM17.50 or higher. What is the main risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "The order activates but may remain unfilled because the price limit cannot be satisfied."], ["B", "The stock must be sold at exactly RM18."], ["C", "The broker must guarantee execution at RM17.50."], ["D", "The instruction automatically converts into a market buy order."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A stop-limit adds execution-price protection but sacrifices certainty of execution. Once activated, the sell limit will not accept prices below RM17.50, so the investor can remain exposed if the market trades lower.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Stop-market prioritizes exit; stop-limit prioritizes price protection but can fail to exit.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An investor has sold a stock short at RM50 and wants an order designed to initiate repurchasing if the share price rises to RM60. Which order best fits the chapter's discussion?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sell limit at RM60"], ["B", "Sell stop at RM40"], ["C", "Buy stop at RM60"], ["D", "Buy limit at RM40"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes that stop orders can also be used to buy securities to limit risk on short sales. Since rising prices hurt the short seller, a buy stop above the current price can trigger an attempt to close the short.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A long position is commonly protected with a sell stop; a short position can be protected with a buy stop.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 26, "question": "<p>An investor buys 100 shares at RM10 each and later sells them at RM10.30. Brokerage commission is RM15 on purchase and RM15 on sale. Ignoring taxes and other charges, what is the net trading profit?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM30 profit"], ["B", "RM15 profit"], ["C", "RM30 loss"], ["D", "RM0"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Gross capital gain is 100 x (RM10.30 - RM10.00) = RM30. The two brokerage commissions total RM30. Net profit is therefore RM0, illustrating how trading costs can consume apparent gains.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive gross trading gain does not necessarily produce a positive net return.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investor suffers a substantial stock-market loss but the broker remains financially sound and the securities are properly accounted for. The investor demands SIPC reimbursement simply because the investment lost value. Which statement best follows the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "SIPC guarantees investors against ordinary market declines."], ["B", "SIPC protection against certain broker failures is not insurance against poor market performance."], ["C", "SIPC covers every loss caused by an inaccurate share-price forecast."], ["D", "Broker registration converts all investment losses into insured claims."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes SIPC as protecting against certain broker financial failures, not guaranteeing investors against bad advice, churning or ordinary investment losses. The historical slide's coverage figures should not be treated as automatically current.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Protection against an intermediary's failure is not protection against the securities' market risk.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A client and broker are resolving a dispute. Process A involves an informal, voluntary attempt to reach agreement. Process B involves presenting arguments to a panel whose decision is binding if the parties have agreed to binding arbitration. Which classification is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A = arbitration; B = mediation"], ["B", "Both processes are ordinary securities trades"], ["C", "A = mediation; B = arbitration"], ["D", "Neither process concerns investor protection"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes mediation as informal and voluntary, with no binding outcome if agreement fails. Arbitration is a more formal panel-based dispute process, with binding consequences where applicable.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Mediation facilitates agreement; arbitration can result in a binding adjudicated decision.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 29, "question": "<p>An investment advisor is properly registered and holds several professional qualifications. An investor therefore assumes the advisor's recommendations must generate positive returns. Which response is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Registration and qualifications are relevant, but neither guarantees investment competence or profitable outcomes."], ["B", "Registration guarantees that all recommended securities will rise."], ["C", "An advisor's professional designation eliminates market risk."], ["D", "The investor should ignore the advisor's track record and incentives."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter explicitly states that regulatory registration does not guarantee competence. Investors should also consider experience, reputation, track record and whether the advisor understands their circumstances.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Regulatory compliance, professional competence and investment performance are separate concepts.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A group of investors forms a legal partnership, pools capital, shares research and votes on investment recommendations. Which practice would most improve the quality of its decisions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Purchase every security recommended by a majority without reviewing evidence."], ["B", "Concentrate all money in securities promoted by the most confident member."], ["C", "Increase trade frequency whenever members disagree."], ["D", "Require members to support recommendations with verifiable evidence, risk analysis and clear investment objectives."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes investment clubs as partnerships pooling knowledge and funds to analyze investment opportunities. A disciplined, evidence-based process helps the club make better use of shared research instead of relying only on group confidence.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A collective investment decision is not automatically a well-informed one.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor can receive RM1,000 today or RM1,080 one year from now. Assuming an available risk-free annual return of 8% and no other differences, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,000 today is worth more."], ["B", "RM1,080 next year is worth more."], ["C", "The two alternatives are financially equivalent."], ["D", "The alternatives cannot be compared using interest rates."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>RM1,000 today can grow to RM1,000 x 1.08 = RM1,080 after one year. They are equivalent at an 8% discount rate under the question's assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare cash flows at the same point in time rather than their nominal amounts.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 2, "question": "<p>RM1,000 is deposited at 10% simple interest per year for three years. What is its future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,300"], ["B", "RM1,331"], ["C", "RM1,210"], ["D", "RM1,100"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Simple interest is earned only on the original principal. Annual interest = RM1,000 x 10% = RM100. After three years, the balance is RM1,000 + 3(RM100) = RM1,300.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not compound interest when the question explicitly specifies simple interest.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 3, "question": "<p>The same RM1,000 is invested at 10% compounded annually for three years. What is the future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,300"], ["B", "RM1,331"], ["C", "RM1,330"], ["D", "RM1,210"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>FV = RM1,000(1.10)^3 = RM1,331. Unlike simple interest, previously accumulated interest earns additional interest.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> RM1,300 is the simple-interest answer, not the compound-interest answer.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 4, "question": "<p>An investor requires 10% annually. How much should they deposit today to receive RM1,210 exactly two years from now?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,100"], ["B", "RM1,210"], ["C", "RM1,000"], ["D", "RM1,331"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV = RM1,210/(1.10)^2 = RM1,000. Present value is obtained by reversing the compounding process.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Future money must be discounted, not multiplied by the compound growth factor.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 5, "question": "<p>RM3,000 is invested for four years at 7% compounded annually. What will it be worth at the end?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM3,840.00"], ["B", "RM3,210.00"], ["C", "RM3,932.39"], ["D", "RM4,207.00"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>FV = RM3,000(1.07)^4 = RM3,932.39. The exponent represents four compounding periods.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> RM3,840 is the simple-interest result; the question specifies compounding.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Two analysts value the same fixed future cash flow, but Analyst A uses a 5% discount rate and Analyst B uses 12%. Which conclusion is correct, all else equal?</p>", "options": [["A", "Analyst B obtains a higher present value."], ["B", "Both must obtain identical present values."], ["C", "The discount rate cannot affect present value."], ["D", "Analyst A obtains a higher present value."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV = FV/(1+r)^n. A higher discount rate increases the denominator and therefore reduces the present value of a positive future cash flow.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compounding and discounting respond in opposite directions to increasing rates.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Which cash-flow pattern qualifies as an ordinary annuity?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,000 at the end of every year for five years"], ["B", "RM1,000 immediately followed by RM1,000 annually starting next year"], ["C", "Unequal payments at the end of every year"], ["D", "RM1,000 at irregular dates"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An ordinary annuity consists of equal cash flows occurring at equal intervals, with each payment at the end of the period. A payment immediately at time zero does not follow the ordinary-annuity timing convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Check both equality of cash flows and when each payment occurs.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 8, "question": "<p>An investor deposits RM1,000 at the end of each year for three years into an account earning 10% annually. What is the future value at the end of Year 3?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM3,000"], ["B", "RM3,310"], ["C", "RM3,641"], ["D", "RM3,300"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>FV = RM1,000[(1.10^3 - 1)/0.10] = RM3,310. Alternatively, RM1,000(1.10)^2 + RM1,000(1.10) + RM1,000 = RM3,310.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The deposit at the end of Year 3 has zero additional years to compound.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 9, "question": "<p>An investment pays RM110 at the end of Year 1 and RM110 at the end of Year 2. What is the present value at a 10% annual discount rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM200.00"], ["B", "RM181.82"], ["C", "RM190.91"], ["D", "RM220.00"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV = RM110/1.10 + RM110/(1.10)^2 = RM100 + RM90.91 = RM190.91. The two payments have different discounting horizons.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Equal cash flows do not have equal present values when received at different dates.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 10, "question": "<p>An investment pays RM100 in Year 1, RM200 in Year 2 and RM300 in Year 3. Using a 10% annual discount rate, what is its present value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM600.00"], ["B", "RM545.45"], ["C", "RM500.00"], ["D", "RM481.59"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV = 100/1.10 + 200/(1.10)^2 + 300/(1.10)^3 = RM481.59. Unequal payments are discounted individually before summing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not use the ordinary-annuity formula because these cash flows are unequal.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 11, "question": "<p>You must choose between RM1,000 immediately and RM1,200 in three years. Assuming an 8% annual required return and otherwise identical risk, which is financially preferable?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,200 in three years, because its nominal amount is higher."], ["B", "RM1,000 immediately, because RM1,200 in three years has a present value of about RM952.60."], ["C", "Both are equivalent at an 8% rate."], ["D", "The answer depends only on the investor's age."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV of RM1,200 = RM1,200/(1.08)^3 ≈ RM952.60, which is less than RM1,000 now. Therefore the immediate payment is more valuable at the stated required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A larger future payment can have a smaller present value.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Investor A invests RM8,000 immediately. Investor B invests RM2,000 at each year-end for four years. Both earn the same positive compound return. At the end of Year 4, who has more wealth?</p>", "options": [["A", "Investor A"], ["B", "Investor B"], ["C", "Both must have identical balances because total deposits are RM8,000."], ["D", "Neither can earn interest under these deposit arrangements."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Investor A's entire RM8,000 earns interest for four years. Investor B's four payments have three, two, one and zero years to compound respectively. Earlier cash flows gain more compounding time.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Equal total contributions do not imply equal future values.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 13, "question": "<p>RM1,000 is invested at a nominal annual rate of 8%, compounded semiannually, for two years. What is the future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,160.00"], ["B", "RM1,166.40"], ["C", "RM1,169.86"], ["D", "RM1,188.10"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The periodic rate is 8%/2 = 4% and there are 2 x 2 = 4 periods. FV = RM1,000(1.04)^4 = RM1,169.86.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Adjust both the interest rate per period and the number of periods.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 14, "question": "<p>A savings product quotes 12% nominal annual interest compounded monthly. What is the approximate effective annual rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "12.00%"], ["B", "12.36%"], ["C", "13.00%"], ["D", "12.68%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>EAR = (1 + 0.12/12)^12 - 1 = (1.01)^12 - 1 ≈ 12.68%. Monthly compounding creates interest on interest within the year.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not compare quoted nominal rates without accounting for compounding frequency.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 15, "question": "<p>An investment compounds monthly for five years at a nominal annual interest rate of 6%. Which N and periodic interest-rate inputs are appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "N = 5; I = 6%"], ["B", "N = 60; I = 0.5%"], ["C", "N = 60; I = 6%"], ["D", "N = 12; I = 0.5%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>There are 12 periods per year for five years, so N = 60. The monthly periodic rate is 6%/12 = 0.5%. This is the setup when using a calculator's periodic-rate convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Never mix an annual rate with a monthly number of periods.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 16, "question": "<p>An investment pays RM1,000 immediately at time zero, then RM1,000 at the end of Years 1 and 2. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is a mixed stream because no two payments are equal."], ["B", "It is a standard ordinary annuity starting at Year 1."], ["C", "It is an annuity-due pattern rather than an ordinary annuity."], ["D", "It has no time-value-of-money effect because all amounts are equal."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Payments at time zero and at the beginning of subsequent periods form an annuity-due timing pattern. The appendix explicitly defines ordinary annuities as payments at period-end; applying that formula without timing adjustment would misvalue the stream.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The appendix focuses on ordinary annuities. Annuity-due terminology is an additional classification for this timing pattern.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 17, "question": "<p>A financial calculator or spreadsheet treats an initial investment as a cash outflow and a future payout as a cash inflow. Which sign convention is consistent with that perspective?</p>", "options": [["A", "Initial investment negative; future payout positive"], ["B", "Both amounts negative"], ["C", "Initial investment positive; future payout negative, regardless of perspective"], ["D", "Both amounts must be positive"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>From the investor's perspective, committing capital is a negative cash flow and receiving proceeds is positive. Opposite signs permit financial functions to represent transfers through time consistently.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A negative PV is not necessarily a bad investment; it often indicates an initial cash outflow.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 18, "question": "<p>An investment requires RM5,000 today and pays RM3,000 at the end of each of the next two years. The required return is 10%. Which decision follows present-value analysis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Reject, because the investment has two payments rather than one."], ["B", "Accept, because the PV of benefits is about RM5,206.61, exceeding the cost."], ["C", "Reject, because the first RM3,000 is less than RM5,000."], ["D", "Accept only if its nominal benefits exceed RM7,000."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV of benefits = 3,000/1.10 + 3,000/(1.10)^2 = RM5,206.61. NPV = RM5,206.61 - RM5,000 = +RM206.61. Under the assumed discount rate and cash flows, benefits exceed costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The decision rule is based on discounted benefits relative to present costs, not arbitrary nominal thresholds.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 19, "question": "<p>The appendix's mixed-stream example pays 30, 40, 50, 60 and 70 at successive year-ends. Discounted at 8%, what is its approximate total present value?</p>", "options": [["A", "250.00"], ["B", "220.00"], ["C", "210.38"], ["D", "193.51"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Table 4A.5 reports individual discounted values of approximately 27.78, 34.29, 39.69, 44.10 and 47.64, adding to approximately 193.51 using the table's underlying values.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Summing the undiscounted payments produces 250, not the present value.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 20, "question": "<p>Two otherwise identical investments quote the same positive nominal annual interest rate, but one compounds annually and the other monthly. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The monthly-compounded investment has a higher effective annual rate, with diminishing incremental gains as compounding becomes increasingly frequent."], ["B", "Both must have the same effective annual rate."], ["C", "Annual compounding necessarily produces higher final wealth."], ["D", "Increasing compounding frequency makes interest simple instead of compound."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>More frequent compounding lets accumulated interest earn interest sooner, raising the effective rate for a fixed positive nominal rate. The appendix's compounding table shows that the incremental benefit becomes smaller at higher frequencies.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The quoted rate and effective return are not identical when compounding frequencies differ.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 21, "question": "<p>A share is purchased for RM100, pays RM4 dividends during the year and ends at RM108. What is the holding-period return?</p>", "options": [["A", "8%"], ["B", "4%"], ["C", "12%"], ["D", "108%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>HPR = [RM4 + (RM108 - RM100)]/RM100 = RM12/RM100 = 12%. It includes both investment income and capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A share's price return alone is not necessarily its total return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Investment A begins at RM1,000, pays RM80 income and ends at RM1,100. Investment B begins at RM1,000, pays RM120 income and ends at RM960. Which investment has the greater total return?</p>", "options": [["A", "B, because RM120 income exceeds RM80"], ["B", "Both have identical total return because starting values are equal"], ["C", "Neither can be evaluated because their income differs"], ["D", "A, because its RM180 total return exceeds B's RM80"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A earns RM80 income plus RM100 capital gain = RM180. B earns RM120 income minus RM40 capital loss = RM80. This mirrors the chapter's comparison of income and capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher cash income does not guarantee higher total return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 23, "question": "<p>An investment earns a 9% nominal annual return while inflation is 5%. Using the chapter's approximate real-return formula, what is the real return?</p>", "options": [["A", "14%"], ["B", "4%"], ["C", "9%"], ["D", "3.81%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's approximation is nominal return minus inflation, giving 9% - 5% = 4%. The exact inflation-adjusted return would differ slightly, but the question explicitly requests the slide's approximation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse the approximate real-return formula with the exact Fisher adjustment.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Suppose the real rate is 2%, expected inflation premium is 3%, and the investment-specific risk premium is 4%. What required return follows the chapter's additive model?</p>", "options": [["A", "9%"], ["B", "7%"], ["C", "5%"], ["D", "4%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Required return = real rate + expected inflation premium + risk premium = 2% + 3% + 4% = 9%. The first two components sum to the risk-free rate of 5%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not omit the inflation component or confuse the risk premium with total required return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An analyst forecasts a 9% expected return for an investment. Given its risk, the investor requires 12%. Which conclusion is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The investment must be accepted because expected return is positive."], ["B", "The investor must ignore the required return."], ["C", "Expected compensation is inadequate relative to the required hurdle."], ["D", "The asset is certain to lose 3%."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Expected return is a forecast, whereas required return is the minimum compensation demanded for risk. An expected return of 9% does not meet a 12% hurdle. This does not mean the realized return must be negative.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A shortfall against the hurdle is not a guaranteed cash loss.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Investment A gains 40% over five years. Investment B gains 30% over one year. Neither pays interim income. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A necessarily performed better because 40% exceeds 30%."], ["B", "A's annual compound return is about 6.96%, while B earned 30% in one year."], ["C", "The investments' annual rates cannot be calculated."], ["D", "Both investments have identical annualized performance."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A's CAGR is (1.40)^(1/5) - 1 ≈ 6.96%. B's one-year rate is 30%. Holding-period returns over different lengths should not be compared without considering time.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A larger cumulative return over a much longer period may correspond to a lower annual compound rate.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investment costs RM1,000 and is expected to pay RM1,400 exactly five years later, with no interim cash flows. What is its compound annual yield?</p>", "options": [["A", "8.00%"], ["B", "40.00%"], ["C", "5.00%"], ["D", "6.96%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>IRR = (1,400/1,000)^(1/5) - 1 ≈ 6.96%. The 40% is the total cumulative appreciation, not the annual yield.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not divide cumulative return by five and assume that is the compound annual rate.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A company's annual dividend rose from RM0.92 to RM1.85 over nine years. What approximate compound annual growth rate does this represent?</p>", "options": [["A", "8.07%"], ["B", "11.23%"], ["C", "101.09%"], ["D", "5.56%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>g = (1.85/0.92)^(1/9) - 1 ≈ 8.07%. This is the same dividend-growth illustration developed in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Total percentage growth and compound annual growth are different measurements.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 29, "question": "<p>An investment costs RM1,000 now and produces RM600 at the end of Year 1 and RM600 at the end of Year 2. What is the approximate IRR?</p>", "options": [["A", "10.00%"], ["B", "20.00%"], ["C", "13.07%"], ["D", "6.00%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Solve 1,000 = 600/(1+r) + 600/(1+r)^2. The discount rate that equates these cash flows to the investment cost is approximately 13.07%. Simply dividing the RM200 nominal surplus by the investment amount ignores the timing of both receipts.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> For a stream of cash flows, IRR requires solving a present-value equation rather than using a simple cumulative return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 30, "question": "<p>The chapter assumes interim investment income is reinvested at the original investment's IRR when interpreting a fully compounded return. If actual reinvestment opportunities earn materially less, what is the main implication?</p>", "options": [["A", "The investment's original coupon automatically increases."], ["B", "The realized terminal wealth from reinvesting income may be lower than that assumption implies."], ["C", "The initial purchase price must fall to zero."], ["D", "The investment necessarily becomes risk-free."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides emphasize interest on interest and the reinvestment rate. Earning less on interim distributions than the assumed compounding rate reduces the wealth accumulated from those distributions. This concerns the chapter's fully compounded wealth interpretation; IRR itself remains a rate solving the original cash-flow equation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A rate calculated from cash flows is not automatically the rate available when reinvesting those cash flows.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 31, "question": "<p>An investor buys shares at RM50 and still owns them when their market price reaches RM75. No shares have been sold. How should the RM25 per-share appreciation be classified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Realized interest income"], ["B", "A guaranteed dividend"], ["C", "A realized capital gain from completed sale"], ["D", "An unrealized or paper capital gain"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A paper or unrealized gain reflects appreciation in an asset still held. A realized capital gain generally arises when the position is sold above its cost, ignoring other tax-specific rules.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Unrealized does not mean the change in market value is economically imaginary.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 32, "question": "<p>An investor demands a higher expected return whenever they are asked to accept greater risk. Which risk attitude best describes this investor?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk-averse"], ["B", "Risk-indifferent"], ["C", "Risk-seeking"], ["D", "Risk-free"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines a risk-averse investor as one who requires greater return to accept greater risk. Risk indifference means required return does not change with risk; risk seeking involves accepting lower return in exchange for more risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk aversion does not mean refusing all risky investments.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 33, "question": "<p>Two otherwise comparable manufacturers face identical product demand. One has financed most operations with debt and faces large compulsory interest payments. Which risk is especially elevated for the heavily indebted firm?</p>", "options": [["A", "Currency risk only"], ["B", "Liquidity risk only"], ["C", "Financial risk arising from capital structure"], ["D", "No additional risk because operating demand is identical"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter associates financial risk with the firm's financing mix, especially debt obligations. Both companies may face similar operating or business risk, but the leveraged company has additional financial commitments.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Business risk concerns operating performance; financial risk concerns the financing structure and repayment obligations.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 34, "question": "<p>An investor receives a fixed annual bond coupon. Inflation unexpectedly rises substantially while the nominal coupon remains unchanged. What risk has most directly materialized?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only business risk"], ["B", "Purchasing-power risk"], ["C", "Only exchange-rate risk"], ["D", "Underwriting risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Unexpected inflation reduces the real value of fixed nominal cash flows. The chapter identifies fixed-income securities and bank deposits as exposed to purchasing-power risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A constant cash payment may buy less over time.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 35, "question": "<p>A fixed-rate bond's market value falls when market interest rates rise. Which interpretation best describes this phenomenon?</p>", "options": [["A", "An increase in the bond's fixed coupon"], ["B", "A direct reduction in the bond's face amount"], ["C", "A guarantee of issuer default"], ["D", "Interest-rate risk affecting the market value of existing fixed cash flows"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>As market rates increase, existing fixed coupons become less attractive relative to newly issued debt, so the market price of the old bond generally declines. The chapter also includes unfavorable reinvestment conditions in its discussion of interest-rate risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Separate fixed promised coupons from changing market prices.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 36, "question": "<p>A property valued at RM900,000 under ordinary market conditions can only be sold immediately by accepting RM650,000. Which risk is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Liquidity risk"], ["B", "Purchasing-power risk"], ["C", "Interest-rate risk alone"], ["D", "Corporate debt risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Liquidity risk is the possibility that an asset cannot be converted to cash conveniently at a reasonable price. A substantial urgent-sale discount indicates limited market liquidity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An asset may be valuable but difficult to sell without sacrificing price.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 37, "question": "<p>A severe hurricane causes a major insurance company to report unexpected losses, immediately reducing its share price. Which risk type best describes the direct company-specific shock?</p>", "options": [["A", "Interest-rate risk"], ["B", "Event risk"], ["C", "Ordinary currency conversion risk"], ["D", "Tax risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies unexpected events, including major hurricanes affecting insurance companies, as examples of event risk because they cause significant and unusually immediate changes in investment value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A sudden event affecting a particular issuer is not automatically the same as a broad market downturn.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 38, "question": "<p>A broad stock market decline occurs following a major deterioration in economic conditions, affecting many companies simultaneously. Which category is most directly represented?</p>", "options": [["A", "Company-specific product liability risk"], ["B", "Only individual management risk"], ["C", "Market risk"], ["D", "Ordinary liquidity preference"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Market risk arises from broader market factors independent of any single investment, such as economy-wide downturns or political upheaval. The same event can influence many security returns.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Differentiate broad economic exposure from a shock unique to one company.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 39, "question": "<p>A Malaysian investor holds foreign securities. Their MYR value declines due to exchange-rate movement, and a new unfavorable tax rule lowers after-tax investment returns. Which two risk categories are illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business risk and financial risk"], ["B", "Event risk and liquidity risk exclusively"], ["C", "Interest-rate risk and underwriting risk"], ["D", "Currency exchange risk and tax risk"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Exchange-rate changes influence the home-currency value of foreign holdings, while unfavorable tax-law changes can reduce after-tax returns and security valuations. The chapter identifies these as separate sources of risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The same investment can be affected by several risk sources at once.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 40, "question": "<p>Investment A has an average return of 10% and standard deviation of 20%. Investment B has an average return of 20% and standard deviation of 30%. Assuming positive average returns, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A has lower absolute volatility, but B has lower relative dispersion measured by CV."], ["B", "B has lower absolute volatility and higher CV."], ["C", "Both investments have the same coefficient of variation."], ["D", "A has lower coefficient of variation because its standard deviation is smaller."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A has σ = 20% and CV = 20/10 = 2.0. B has σ = 30% and CV = 30/20 = 1.5. A has lower total standard deviation, while B has less standard deviation per unit of average return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Standard deviation measures absolute dispersion; coefficient of variation evaluates relative dispersion.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor's primary objective is to receive regular dividend and interest payments rather than maximize long-term share-price appreciation. Which portfolio type best matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Growth-oriented portfolio"], ["B", "Income-oriented portfolio"], ["C", "Speculative portfolio concentrated in derivatives"], ["D", "Portfolio selected solely by historical beta"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter distinguishes income-oriented portfolios, which emphasize regular interest and dividend income, from growth-oriented portfolios, which emphasize long-term capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio objectives describe the investor's intended benefits, not simply the number of securities owned.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Portfolio A has an expected return of 8% and standard deviation of 12%. Portfolio B has an expected return of 10% and the same standard deviation. Under the chapter's mean-variance framework, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is superior because it has lower return."], ["B", "Both are equally efficient because their risks are identical."], ["C", "B dominates A because it offers more expected return for the same measured risk."], ["D", "A must be preferred by every risk-averse investor."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An efficient portfolio offers the highest expected return for a given level of risk. Since B provides more expected return with identical standard deviation, A is dominated on these measures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk aversion does not justify selecting a lower-return portfolio when another portfolio has exactly the same measured risk.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 3, "question": "<p>An investor allocates 50% to Stock A with an expected return of 12%, 30% to Stock B at 8%, and 20% to bonds at 5%. What is the portfolio's expected return?</p>", "options": [["A", "9.4%"], ["B", "8.33%"], ["C", "12.0%"], ["D", "8.0%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>E(Rp) = 0.50(12%) + 0.30(8%) + 0.20(5%) = 6% + 2.4% + 1% = 9.4%. Portfolio expected return is a weighted average of constituent expected returns.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply portfolio weights instead of taking the simple arithmetic mean of individual expected returns.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Stock A has an expected return of 12%, while Stock B has an expected return of 8%. An investor wants a fully invested two-stock portfolio with an expected return of 11%. What proportion must be allocated to A?</p>", "options": [["A", "25%"], ["B", "50%"], ["C", "60%"], ["D", "75%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Let w be Stock A's weight. Then 11% = 12%w + 8%(1 - w), so 3% = 4%w and w = 0.75. The portfolio therefore requires 75% in A and 25% in B.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio weights must add to 100%; solve for the unknown weight rather than averaging the returns.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 5, "question": "<p>Why is portfolio standard deviation generally not equal to the weighted average of individual stock standard deviations?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because portfolio weights cannot be expressed as percentages."], ["B", "Because portfolio risk also depends on how asset returns move together."], ["C", "Because all portfolios have a standard deviation of zero."], ["D", "Because diversification necessarily raises expected returns."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter explicitly states that portfolio standard deviation depends on weights, individual standard deviations and correlations. Correlation determines whether individual price movements reinforce or offset one another.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Expected return is a weighted average; portfolio standard deviation generally is not.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Two securities have a return correlation coefficient of -0.80. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their returns tend to move in opposite directions strongly."], ["B", "Their returns must be identical."], ["C", "Both securities must generate negative expected returns."], ["D", "Their portfolio weights are automatically equal."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A strongly negative correlation indicates that the two return series tend to move in opposite directions. Correlation measures co-movement, not average return, allocation or guaranteed performance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Negative correlation does not mean negative investment returns.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Two assets have standard deviations of 20% and 10%, respectively. They are equally weighted and perfectly positively correlated (ρ = +1). What is the portfolio standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "10%"], ["B", "11.18%"], ["C", "15%"], ["D", "30%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With perfect positive correlation and nonnegative weights, portfolio standard deviation equals 0.5(20%) + 0.5(10%) = 15%. No diversification reduction occurs relative to the weighted average in this special case.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The weighted-average risk shortcut works here because correlation equals +1, not because the portfolio has two assets.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Two equally weighted assets have standard deviations of 20% and 10%, with zero correlation. What is the portfolio's approximate standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "15.00%"], ["B", "5.00%"], ["C", "10.00%"], ["D", "11.18%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>σp = sqrt[(0.5² x 0.20²) + (0.5² x 0.10²)] = sqrt(0.0125) = 0.1118, or 11.18%. The covariance term is zero because the correlation coefficient equals zero.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Zero correlation does not mean zero portfolio risk.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Two equally weighted assets have standard deviations of 20% and 10%. Their correlation is -0.50. What is the portfolio's approximate standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "8.66%"], ["B", "11.18%"], ["C", "15.00%"], ["D", "5.00%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Portfolio variance = 0.5²(0.20²) + 0.5²(0.10²) + 2(0.5)(0.5)(0.20)(0.10)(-0.5) = 0.0075. Taking the square root gives 8.66%. Negative covariance reduces risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The negative correlation term must be subtracted through the covariance component; do not simply subtract 0.50 from a standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Two assets have σA = 20%, σB = 10%, and perfect negative correlation. Assuming only positive portfolio weights and full investment, what weights would eliminate portfolio variance?</p>", "options": [["A", "50% A and 50% B"], ["B", "One-third A and two-thirds B"], ["C", "Two-thirds A and one-third B"], ["D", "100% A and 0% B"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With ρ = -1, the risk terms cancel when wAσA = wBσB. Thus 0.20wA = 0.10wB, with wA + wB = 1, giving wA = 1/3 and wB = 2/3. This is the mathematical condition underlying the slide's perfect-negative-correlation principle.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Perfect negative correlation permits complete risk cancellation with appropriate weights, not necessarily with equal weights.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 11, "question": "<p>Three otherwise identical two-asset portfolios have return correlations of +0.90, +0.20 and -0.60. Their weights and individual asset volatilities are identical and positive. Which portfolio has the lowest standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio with +0.90 correlation"], ["B", "The portfolio with +0.20 correlation"], ["C", "The portfolio with -0.60 correlation"], ["D", "All must have the same portfolio volatility"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Holding asset volatilities and positive weights constant, decreasing correlation reduces the covariance component and therefore reduces portfolio variance. The -0.60 portfolio has the lowest risk of the three.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare correlation while holding other variables constant; otherwise the conclusion may not follow.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Asset A earns 20% in a strong economy and 0% in a weak economy. Asset B earns 0% in a strong economy and 20% in a weak economy. Both states are equally likely. If the assets are equally weighted, what portfolio return occurs in each state?</p>", "options": [["A", "20% in both states"], ["B", "20% in the strong state and 0% in the weak state"], ["C", "0% in both states"], ["D", "10% in both states"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In either state, the portfolio earns 0.5(20%) + 0.5(0%) = 10%. The opposing returns cancel the variation in this simplified two-state example.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An individually volatile asset can contribute to a stable portfolio when its movements offset those of another asset.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 13, "question": "<p>In the chapter's IBM-Celgene example, Celgene has a much higher individual standard deviation than IBM, yet a portfolio holding approximately 86% IBM and 14% Celgene has a lower standard deviation than IBM alone. What does this illustrate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Celgene must be risk-free."], ["B", "Adding a highly volatile asset can reduce total portfolio risk when its covariance benefits are sufficient."], ["C", "Portfolio standard deviation is always equal to the smaller asset's standard deviation."], ["D", "The higher-returning asset always eliminates all volatility."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Table 5.1 reports approximately 28.6% volatility for IBM, 58.3% for Celgene and 25.3% for their 86/14 portfolio. The portfolio outcome reflects the co-movement of their returns, not just individual volatility.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Evaluate a new asset by its effect on portfolio risk, not solely by its standalone volatility.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 14, "question": "<p>In Table 5.3, Asset X returns rise from 8% to 16% while Asset Y's returns fall from 24% to 8%. What is the standard deviation of a portfolio containing two-thirds X and one-third Y across the five scenarios shown?</p>", "options": [["A", "0%"], ["B", "3.2%"], ["C", "4.2%"], ["D", "6.3%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>For every year in Table 5.3, (2/3)RX + (1/3)RY gives approximately 13.33%. Because the portfolio return is constant across the shown scenarios, its standard deviation is zero.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not assume that positive individual standard deviations necessarily imply positive portfolio standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Table 5.3 reports approximately the same average return for portfolios XY and XZ, but standard deviations of 0% and 4.2%, respectively. Which portfolio dominates under the chapter's stated risk-return measures?</p>", "options": [["A", "XZ, because higher risk is always superior."], ["B", "Both, because mean returns are equal."], ["C", "XY, because it provides comparable average return with lower volatility."], ["D", "Neither can be assessed even under the chapter's two stated metrics."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>XY and XZ have average returns of approximately 13.3%, but XY's standard deviation is zero in the given example while XZ has 4.2%. XY offers the better measured risk-return combination.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher risk level is not itself desirable when no additional expected return is provided.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A Malaysian equity portfolio is concentrated in domestic industries. The investor considers adding securities from economies whose business cycles are not perfectly aligned with Malaysia's. What is the strongest justification?</p>", "options": [["A", "Foreign investments cannot experience recessions."], ["B", "Owning foreign securities guarantees higher returns."], ["C", "Currency differences eliminate all investment risks."], ["D", "Different economic-cycle exposures may provide diversification benefits."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter emphasizes that foreign markets may not move together with domestic markets, creating diversification opportunities. The benefit depends on return relationships and does not guarantee higher returns or protection from global crises.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> International diversification can reduce some exposures without eliminating common global risk.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 17, "question": "<p>A foreign stock rises 10% in its local currency, but its currency depreciates 15% against the investor's home currency. What is the most appropriate interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The home-currency return must be +10%."], ["B", "The home-currency return is approximately -6.5%, demonstrating currency exchange risk."], ["C", "Currency changes cannot affect stock investors."], ["D", "The home-currency return must be +25%."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The combined home-currency return is (1.10 x 0.85) - 1 = -0.065, or -6.5%. The chapter identifies currency exchange risk as a disadvantage of international diversification. The numerical combination applies that concept.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Foreign investment performance must be evaluated in the investor's home currency when measuring their actual return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 18, "question": "<p>Which approach is specifically described by the chapter as typically not being considered a true international investment for diversification purposes?</p>", "options": [["A", "Purchasing shares of a U.S. multinational company with substantial overseas operations"], ["B", "Investing in a mutual fund that holds foreign company stocks"], ["C", "Purchasing American Depositary Shares"], ["D", "Directly holding foreign company equity"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes that investing in U.S. multinational companies is typically not classified as true international investing for diversification purposes, even though multinational businesses may have foreign operations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A domestic company earning revenue overseas is not structurally identical to directly holding securities of foreign issuers.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 19, "question": "<p>One company's share price falls sharply after a lawsuit against its management, while the overall stock market is relatively unaffected. What type of risk is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Systematic market risk"], ["B", "Interest-rate risk affecting all bonds"], ["C", "Unsystematic firm-specific risk"], ["D", "A guaranteed risk-free return"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies lawsuits and labor strikes as examples of unsystematic risk, since they are primarily firm-specific and may be reduced through diversification.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A large loss is not automatically systematic risk; identify whether the cause affects the market broadly or the particular company.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 20, "question": "<p>An investor holds shares in 100 companies across multiple industries. An economy-wide financial shock causes most stock prices to decline together. Which conclusion best follows Chapter 5?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio must be risk-free because it owns many securities."], ["B", "Only firm-specific risk can explain broad simultaneous declines."], ["C", "The portfolio was necessarily fraudulent because prices fell."], ["D", "Diversification cannot fully eliminate systematic risk arising from common market forces."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification can reduce firm-specific unsystematic risk, but systematic risk arises from forces affecting broad groups of investments. The chapter's portfolio-risk diagram shows a residual risk floor as more securities are added.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Many holdings do not imply immunity from broad market conditions.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 21, "question": "<p>What beta value does the chapter assign to the overall market portfolio or its broad market benchmark?</p>", "options": [["A", "0"], ["B", "1.0"], ["C", "-1.0"], ["D", "A value that must always exceed 2.0"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>By construction in the chapter's beta framework, the overall market has beta 1.0. Individual securities can have betas above or below one.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of 1 represents market-level sensitivity, not an expected return of 1%.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Stock A has beta 0.5 and total return standard deviation 40%. Stock B has beta 1.3 and total standard deviation 25%. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A has greater total volatility, while B has greater market sensitivity."], ["B", "A is less volatile in every sense because its beta is smaller."], ["C", "B has greater total volatility because its beta exceeds one."], ["D", "Both securities must have identical systematic risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Standard deviation measures overall dispersion of returns, while beta measures exposure to market movements. A has higher standard deviation but lower beta than B.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Low beta is not synonymous with low total standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A stock has beta 1.50. In the chapter's simplified market-sensitivity illustration, what market-related return movement corresponds to a 10% increase in the market?</p>", "options": [["A", "+10%"], ["B", "+5%"], ["C", "+15%"], ["D", "+1.5%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The market-linked component is approximately β x market movement = 1.5 x 10% = 15%. This is the simplified interpretation used in the slides, not a guarantee of the stock's actual realized return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Beta is market sensitivity, not a promise that the security must produce the calculated return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 24, "question": "<p>A security has beta 0.50. Using the chapter's simplified beta interpretation, what market-related movement corresponds to an 8% market decline?</p>", "options": [["A", "-8%"], ["B", "-16%"], ["C", "+4%"], ["D", "-4%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The expected market-linked response is approximately 0.50 x (-8%) = -4%. A lower positive beta implies lower sensitivity to the market's return fluctuations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of 0.5 halves the market-linked movement; it does not reverse its sign.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An asset has a negative beta. Why could it be valuable in a diversified portfolio even if its expected return is relatively low?</p>", "options": [["A", "Negative beta guarantees that the investment's own price can never fall."], ["B", "The asset may provide a hedge against some market movements by tending to move oppositely to the market."], ["C", "Its return is always negative."], ["D", "It automatically eliminates all portfolio risk regardless of weight."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Negative beta means the asset's market-related return sensitivity is opposite in sign to the benchmark. This may offer a diversification or hedging benefit, making the asset valuable despite a potentially lower required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Negative beta does not mean guaranteed profits, negative expected return or complete portfolio insurance.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Why does the chapter treat systematic risk, rather than total standalone volatility, as the relevant positively rewarded risk within the CAPM framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because standard deviation is mathematically meaningless."], ["B", "Because every security has zero firm-specific risk."], ["C", "Because diversified investors can reduce firm-specific risk, leaving market-related risk as the relevant priced component."], ["D", "Because higher volatility always produces a guaranteed higher realized return."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides describe only nondiversifiable risk as relevant to the positive risk-return relationship under CAPM. The reasoning assumes investors can diversify away much of the firm-specific risk of individual securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> CAPM's pricing relationship concerns systematic risk, not guaranteed compensation for every form of uncertainty.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 27, "question": "<p>A portfolio holds 40% in a stock with beta 1.5 and 60% in a stock with beta 0.5. What is the portfolio beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.90"], ["B", "1.00"], ["C", "1.20"], ["D", "0.75"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Portfolio beta is the weighted average of individual betas: βp = 0.40(1.5) + 0.60(0.5) = 0.60 + 0.30 = 0.90.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio beta is a weighted average even though portfolio standard deviation generally is not.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 28, "question": "<p>In Table 5.6, Portfolio V has weights of 10%, 30%, 20%, 20% and 20%, with corresponding betas of 1.65, 1.00, 1.30, 1.10 and 1.25. What is Portfolio V's beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.910"], ["B", "1.500"], ["C", "1.000"], ["D", "1.195"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>βV = 0.10(1.65) + 0.30(1.00) + 0.20(1.30) + 0.20(1.10) + 0.20(1.25) = 1.195. This uses the portfolio beta formula given in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not take the unweighted mean of the constituent betas when allocation weights differ.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 29, "question": "<p>Table 5.6's Portfolio W has weights 10%, 10%, 20%, 10% and 50% and corresponding betas 0.80, 1.00, 0.65, 0.75 and 1.05. What is the portfolio beta, and how does it compare with Portfolio V's beta of 1.195?</p>", "options": [["A", "1.195, identical to V"], ["B", "0.910, indicating less market sensitivity than V"], ["C", "1.050, indicating more sensitivity than V"], ["D", "0.500, meaning it eliminates all market risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>βW = 0.10(0.80) + 0.10(1.00) + 0.20(0.65) + 0.10(0.75) + 0.50(1.05) = 0.910. This is lower than V's beta of 1.195, so W has lower market sensitivity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare portfolio betas to assess relative market sensitivity, not necessarily total standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 30, "question": "<p>The risk-free rate is 4%, expected market return is 10%, and a security has beta 1.5. What is its CAPM-required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "10%"], ["B", "15%"], ["C", "13%"], ["D", "9%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CAPM required return = Rf + β(E[Rm] - Rf) = 4% + 1.5(10% - 4%) = 4% + 9% = 13%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply beta to the market risk premium, not the entire market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 31, "question": "<p>The risk-free rate is 3%, expected market return is 11%, and an investment has beta 0.75. What required return does CAPM imply?</p>", "options": [["A", "9%"], ["B", "8.25%"], ["C", "11%"], ["D", "6%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Market risk premium = 11% - 3% = 8%. Required return = 3% + 0.75(8%) = 9%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of 0.75 indicates 75% of the market risk premium, not 75% of the entire market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 32, "question": "<p>The risk-free rate is 5%, the expected market return is 9%, and CAPM implies that a security requires 13%. What is its beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "1.0"], ["B", "1.5"], ["C", "0.5"], ["D", "2.0"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>13% = 5% + β(9% - 5%). Rearranging gives β = (13% - 5%)/4% = 2.0. Beta can be recovered from the required risk premium and the market risk premium.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> When beta is the unknown, rearrange the CAPM formula rather than guessing from the required return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 33, "question": "<p>The expected market return is 12%, while the risk-free rate is 4%. What is the market risk premium used in CAPM?</p>", "options": [["A", "12%"], ["B", "8%"], ["C", "16%"], ["D", "3%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The market risk premium is the expected market return in excess of the risk-free rate: 12% - 4% = 8%. It forms the slope of the Security Market Line in the standard CAPM framework.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Market risk premium is not the total market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 34, "question": "<p>Assume the risk-free rate is 4%, expected market return is 10%, and a security has beta -0.5. What CAPM-required return follows?</p>", "options": [["A", "7%"], ["B", "4%"], ["C", "1%"], ["D", "-1%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Required return = 4% + (-0.5)(10% - 4%) = 4% - 3% = 1%. Negative market exposure can carry a lower required return because of its potential hedging value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A negative beta does not automatically mean a negative CAPM-required return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 35, "question": "<p>Figure 5.6 shows a 6% risk-free rate and a 10% required return for beta 1.0. According to this SML, what required return corresponds to beta 1.25?</p>", "options": [["A", "11%"], ["B", "12.5%"], ["C", "10.25%"], ["D", "13.5%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The market risk premium is 10% - 6% = 4%. At beta 1.25, the required return is 6% + 1.25(4%) = 11%. This reproduces the relationship illustrated in Figure 5.6.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Read the SML using its intercept and slope rather than multiplying beta directly by the market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 36, "question": "<p>In a standard CAPM Security Market Line, the risk-free rate is held constant while the expected market risk premium increases. What happens to the SML?</p>", "options": [["A", "It becomes horizontal regardless of beta."], ["B", "Its vertical intercept becomes zero."], ["C", "It shifts downward in parallel."], ["D", "It becomes steeper while retaining the same intercept."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The CAPM line has intercept Rf and slope E(Rm) - Rf. Increasing the market risk premium while keeping Rf fixed increases the slope without changing the beta-zero intercept.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish changes to the SML intercept from changes to the slope.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 37, "question": "<p>An asset has beta 1.2. The risk-free rate is 4% and the market's expected return is 10%. Your analysis estimates an expected return of 13%. Where does the asset lie relative to the SML?</p>", "options": [["A", "Below the SML by 1.8 percentage points"], ["B", "Above the SML by 1.8 percentage points"], ["C", "Exactly on the SML"], ["D", "Below the SML by 3 percentage points"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CAPM-required return = 4% + 1.2(6%) = 11.2%. Expected return exceeds this by 13% - 11.2% = 1.8 percentage points, so the asset lies above the SML on these assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive expected alpha is relative to the model and forecast; it is not proof that the estimate will be realized.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 38, "question": "<p>Which comparison correctly distinguishes the efficient frontier from the Security Market Line?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both plot total portfolio standard deviation on their horizontal axes."], ["B", "The efficient frontier plots beta, while the SML plots the number of securities."], ["C", "The efficient frontier considers expected return against portfolio standard deviation, while the SML relates required return to beta."], ["D", "Both graphs demonstrate that return is independent of risk."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The efficient frontier describes attainable efficient portfolio combinations in expected-return/standard-deviation space. The SML graphs CAPM-required return against systematic risk measured by beta.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A graph's horizontal-axis risk measure is often the easiest way to distinguish these two concepts.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 39, "question": "<p>Portfolio A offers 10% expected return at 15% standard deviation. Portfolio B offers 12% expected return at the same 15% standard deviation. Both are feasible under identical assumptions. What should an investor conclude under MPT?</p>", "options": [["A", "B dominates A; A is inefficient on the return-volatility comparison."], ["B", "A is necessarily efficient because its return is lower."], ["C", "Both portfolios must be on the efficient frontier."], ["D", "B must be rejected because higher return always means higher standard deviation."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>B gives a higher expected return at exactly the same standard deviation. Therefore A is dominated and cannot be efficient under the stated mean-variance opportunity set.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An investor who wants lower risk should choose a lower-risk efficient portfolio, not a dominated one.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 40, "question": "<p>An advisor recommends a portfolio of 30 familiar companies and claims it is well diversified solely because it holds many securities. Which improvement best reconciles traditional portfolio management with MPT as recommended by the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose only the stocks with the highest previous-year returns."], ["B", "Ignore the existing holdings and buy more companies from the same industry."], ["C", "Replace every asset with the lowest-beta security regardless of investor objectives."], ["D", "Evaluate expected returns, asset weights, standard deviations, correlations and portfolio beta, then select an allocation suited to the investor's risk tolerance."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's recommended reconciliation combines practical diversification across assets and industries with MPT's statistical analysis. It calls for attention to correlations, portfolio beta, investor risk tolerance and efficient risk-return trade-offs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Holding many securities is a starting point, not evidence that the resulting portfolio has been efficiently constructed.</p>"}];
window.DIFFICULT_BANK = [{"module": 1, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which definition of an investment matches the chapter exactly?</p>", "options": [["A", "Any asset into which funds can be placed with the expectation that it will generate positive income and/or increase its value."], ["B", "Any asset bought with borrowed money in the hope of a capital gain."], ["C", "Any security traded on an organised exchange."], ["D", "Any asset whose price has risen over the past year."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines an investment by the <strong>expectation of positive income and/or an increase in value</strong>. B adds borrowing and drops income; C restricts investments to exchange-traded securities, but property and tangibles can be investments too; D confuses past performance with the defining expectation.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In the chapter's classification of investors, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Individuals are typically net demanders of funds; businesses are net suppliers."], ["B", "Governments are typically net suppliers of funds."], ["C", "All three groups are net suppliers when interest rates are high."], ["D", "Individuals are typically net suppliers of funds; businesses and governments are typically net demanders."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Individuals, as a group, save more than they borrow, while businesses and governments typically need outside funds for production and public projects. \"Net\" describes the group's typical aggregate role: individuals still borrow for houses and cars.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which pair lists the chapter's <strong>two disadvantages</strong> of short-term investments?</p>", "options": [["A", "Low liquidity and high default risk"], ["B", "Price volatility and currency risk"], ["C", "High taxes and high transaction costs"], ["D", "Low levels of return and loss of potential purchasing power from inflation"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides list advantages (high liquidity, low risk of default) and disadvantages (low return, possible loss of purchasing power from inflation). A reverses the advantages; C and B are not the chapter's listed drawbacks.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why do short-term, highly liquid, low-default-risk investments usually offer low returns?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because investors value safety and liquidity, so issuers need to offer less compensation."], ["B", "Because the government caps their interest rates."], ["C", "Because they are taxed more heavily than stocks."], ["D", "Because their prices are more volatile than those of stocks."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Desirable features (safety, liquidity) reduce the compensation investors demand. This is the general logic of the risk-return trade-off: you are paid for accepting undesirable characteristics. B and C are not the chapter's reasoning; D is false, as short-term instruments are less volatile.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An equity mutual fund and an ETF holding the same stocks differ mainly in that:</p>", "options": [["A", "the ETF is a direct investment, while the mutual fund is indirect."], ["B", "the ETF's shares trade on an exchange while the market is open, unlike conventional mutual fund shares."], ["C", "the mutual fund cannot hold more than 30 stocks."], ["D", "only the ETF provides diversification."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both pool investors' money into a professionally managed portfolio (both are indirect and both diversify). The ETF changes the <strong>trading structure</strong>: its shares trade on exchanges during market hours, according to the slides.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the chapter place \"meeting investment prerequisites\" before selecting investments?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because an investor without emergency funds or protection may be forced to sell investments at a bad time, whatever their quality."], ["B", "Because regulators forbid investing without insurance."], ["C", "Because prerequisites raise expected returns."], ["D", "Because selecting investments is the least important step."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The prerequisites (funds for emergencies, protection against death, illness and disability) prevent a mismatch between the investor's circumstances and the asset's characteristics. Without them, a cash need can force a sale during a market fall, the \"survive first\" logic.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A wealth manager describes a client as having \"high risk tolerance but low risk capacity\". What does this mean in practice?</p>", "options": [["A", "The client is psychologically comfortable with volatility but financially unable to absorb large losses, so the portfolio should be constrained by capacity."], ["B", "The client can afford large losses but dislikes them."], ["C", "The client should take maximum risk."], ["D", "The two terms mean the same thing."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Tolerance is psychological willingness; capacity is financial ability (horizon, income stability, buffers, obligations). Professionals generally let the <strong>lower</strong> of the two constrain the portfolio. The chapter's life-cycle logic is mainly about capacity.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A 26-year-old has stable income, a six-month emergency fund, no debt and is saving for retirement in 35 years. Which allocation best fits the chapter's life-cycle framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Mostly short-term deposits to preserve capital."], ["B", "Only options and futures, because young investors should maximise risk."], ["C", "Only bonds, because bond prices never fall."], ["D", "Mostly growth-oriented investments such as common stocks, because the long horizon allows time to recover from falls."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Young investors with the prerequisites met have high risk capacity and a long horizon, so growth assets are relatively suitable. A sacrifices growth needlessly; C is false (bond prices fall when rates rise); B confuses \"can take more risk\" with \"should take maximum risk\".</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor earns an 8% nominal return, pays 25% tax on it, and inflation is 4%. What is the <strong>approximate</strong> real after-tax return?</p>", "options": [["A", "4.0%"], ["B", "6.0%"], ["C", "2.0%"], ["D", "-2.0%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>After-tax return = 8% x (1 - 0.25) = 6%. Real ≈ 6% - 4% = <strong>2%</strong>. A ignores tax; B ignores inflation; D subtracts the tax rate and inflation in the wrong way.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Classify a directly held, newly issued 5-year Malaysian government bond for a Malaysian investor.</p>", "options": [["A", "Security, indirect, equity, short-term, foreign"], ["B", "Property, direct, debt, long-term, domestic"], ["C", "Security, direct, derivative, short-term, domestic"], ["D", "Security, direct, debt, long-term, domestic"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A bond is a <strong>security</strong> (not property), held <strong>directly</strong>, it is <strong>debt</strong> (you lend to the issuer), its maturity exceeds one year (<strong>long-term</strong>), and for a Malaysian investor it is <strong>domestic</strong>.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A Malaysian bank offers a savings account at 2.5% when inflation is 3%. A client says it is \"risk-free\". How should an adviser respond?</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree completely."], ["B", "It is close to free of default and volatility risk, but the expected real return is about -0.5%, so purchasing power is expected to fall."], ["C", "Say the account has high default risk."], ["D", "Say it is riskier than equities in every respect."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Real ≈ 2.5% - 3% = -0.5%. Separating nominal safety from real economic safety is the professional answer.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>Using the chapter's framework, which constraint is most binding for an investor who must pay a known RM200,000 liability in 18 months?</p>", "options": [["A", "Tax constraint"], ["B", "Legal constraint on owning foreign stocks"], ["C", "Time horizon and liquidity constraint"], ["D", "Unique preference for ethical investing"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A known near-term liability makes the horizon short and the liquidity need high, which dominates the choice of assets. The other constraints may matter, but they do not determine suitability here.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two investors buy the same equity fund. Investor A needs the money for a house deposit in 9 months; Investor B is saving for retirement in 30 years. Which statement best analyses the situation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund is equally suitable for both because they hold the same asset."], ["B", "Suitability differs: the same asset suits B's long horizon but exposes A to a possible large fall just before the money is needed."], ["C", "The fund is more suitable for A because A needs higher returns sooner."], ["D", "Neither should invest, because equity funds are risky."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Suitability is a function of objective, horizon, risk capacity and liquidity needs, not of the asset alone. A cannot afford a fall before the purchase; B has time to recover. D replaces analysis with a blanket rule.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An ETF can be sold within seconds on any trading day. Why can it still be <strong>unsuitable</strong> for money needed next month?</p>", "options": [["A", "ETFs are illiquid."], ["B", "ETFs cannot be sold until they mature."], ["C", "Market liquidity is not price stability: the ETF can be sold quickly, but possibly at a much lower value than was invested."], ["D", "ETFs pay no dividends."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines liquidity as quick conversion <strong>with little or no loss in value</strong>. An equity ETF meets the speed condition but not the price-stability one over short horizons. That distinction is what makes it unsuitable for near-term needs.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Market interest rates rise from 5% to 7%. What happens to the price of an existing bond paying a fixed 5% coupon, and why?</p>", "options": [["A", "It rises, because higher rates make all bonds more valuable."], ["B", "It falls only if the issuer's credit rating changes."], ["C", "It stays at par, because coupon payments do not change."], ["D", "It falls, because its fixed 5% coupon must compete with new bonds paying 7%, so buyers pay less for it."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bond's cash flows are fixed; when new bonds offer more, the old bond's price must fall until its return is competitive. Interest rates and bond prices move in <strong>opposite</strong> directions.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>Two bonds are identical except that Bond A matures in 2 years and Bond B in 20 years. Market rates rise by 2 percentage points. Which is most likely?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both fall by the same percentage."], ["B", "Neither changes, because coupons are fixed."], ["C", "Bond A's price falls by more, because it matures sooner."], ["D", "Bond B's price falls by more, because its distant cash flows are discounted over more periods."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter states rates and bond prices move oppositely. Extending its logic with TVM (Appendix 4A): the further away the cash flows, the more a rate change affects their present value, so long bonds are more rate-sensitive.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A start-up founder holds 90% of their wealth in the start-up's shares and asks whether to buy more \"because I believe in the company\". What is the strongest diagnosis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy more; conviction reduces risk."], ["B", "The founder's wealth is already concentrated: their income and wealth depend on the same company, so diversifying away from it reduces unnecessary concentration risk."], ["C", "Sell everything immediately."], ["D", "Concentration only matters for institutional investors."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification is about avoiding unnecessary concentration. A founder's job and wealth are exposed to the same firm-specific risk; belief does not remove that risk. C is an overreaction; a reasoned rebalancing is the answer.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why is age only a <strong>proxy</strong> in life-cycle investing rather than a decision rule?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the real drivers are horizon, human capital (future earnings), obligations and liquidity needs, which correlate with age but differ between individuals."], ["B", "Because older investors always take more risk."], ["C", "Because life cycle stages are defined by income, not age."], ["D", "Because regulators set allocation by age."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Age correlates with time to recover and remaining earnings, but a 25-year-old with dependants and unstable income may have less capacity than a 50-year-old with secure wealth. The chapter's \"always aggressive when young\" trap rests on this.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Why might a household rationally hold short-term deposits paying below inflation instead of only higher-expected-return assets?</p>", "options": [["A", "Households are always irrational."], ["B", "Deposits have higher expected returns than equities."], ["C", "Liquidity and certainty have value: they hedge unexpected spending needs and avoid forced sales at depressed prices, a service worth paying for through a lower expected return."], ["D", "Inflation does not affect deposits."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This generalises the chapter's point that liquidity is economically valuable: the \"cost\" (a negative expected real return) is the price of insurance against liquidity shocks.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Cash is the safest investment, so a retirement fund for someone aged 30 should be held entirely in cash.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: cash has no risk of any kind."], ["B", "Incorrect, because cash has high default risk."], ["C", "Partly right on default risk, volatility and liquidity, but wrong overall: over 30+ years inflation can steadily erode purchasing power, so cash alone is unlikely to be suitable."], ["D", "Correct, because retirement funds must never fluctuate."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Safety is multidimensional. Cash is very safe in nominal terms but carries purchasing-power risk, which matters most over long horizons. A good answer credits what is true and rejects the \"safest in every sense\" claim.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Investment X (expected 15%) is better than Investment Y (expected 7%).\"</p>", "options": [["A", "True, since 15% &gt; 7%."], ["B", "True only if X's taxes are lower."], ["C", "It cannot be judged without comparing the risks taken and the investor's objectives, horizon and liquidity needs."], ["D", "False, because low-return investments are always safer."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Return cannot be evaluated independently of risk and suitability. D swaps one blanket rule for another: a low return does not guarantee low risk.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A broker recommends a high-growth technology fund to a client in their first meeting, without asking about the client's goals or circumstances. What is the strongest criticism?</p>", "options": [["A", "Technology funds are never suitable."], ["B", "The broker skipped the steps that come first (prerequisites, goals and the plan): a product was chosen before suitability was established."], ["C", "The broker should have recommended a derivative instead."], ["D", "There is nothing wrong if the fund's past returns were high."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The seven-step process puts goals and planning before selection; the product should follow the goal. Past performance (D) does not establish suitability.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A fintech app advertises \"invest your spare change automatically into a global ETF\". For a student with no emergency fund, evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Always good: small amounts carry no risk."], ["B", "Diversified and low-cost, but the chapter's order still applies: building an emergency reserve first matters more, and the ETF's value can fall when cash is needed."], ["C", "Bad, because ETFs are illegal for students."], ["D", "Good only if the ETF tracks a single stock."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The product may be sensible, but suitability depends on prerequisites. Small amounts reduce the stakes but do not change the logic that emergency funds come first.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate the claim: \"Because institutional investors manage more money, they are always better investors than individuals.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, size guarantees skill."], ["B", "Incorrect: size brings cost advantages and professional resources, but institutions also face agency problems and constraints (benchmarks, liquidity of large trades), and size does not guarantee better risk-adjusted results."], ["C", "Correct, because they trade on exchanges."], ["D", "Incorrect, because institutions cannot diversify."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's distinction is whose objectives are being optimised, not who is better. Scale has advantages and disadvantages; D is false (institutions diversify easily).</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>A researcher finds that young investors who hold mostly cash end up with lower retirement wealth on average. Which conclusion is <strong>best supported</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "Everyone should hold only equities."], ["B", "Young people should stop saving."], ["C", "Cash causes lower income."], ["D", "On average, the long horizon favours growth assets, but the finding is about averages: individual suitability still depends on prerequisites, capacity and tolerance."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Evidence about averages does not override individual suitability. The answer combines the life-cycle logic with the chapter's insistence that \"always\" rules fail.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>The chapter's tax slides use 2012 U.S. brackets. What is the most defensible way to use them in a Malaysian course?</p>", "options": [["A", "Apply the U.S. percentages to Malaysian investors."], ["B", "Ignore taxes altogether."], ["C", "Treat the numbers as historical course material and transfer only the durable principles: after-tax return, type of income and timing of gains."], ["D", "Assume all countries tax capital gains the same way."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Concepts travel across tax systems; specific rates do not. This is exactly the chapter's own warning about its U.S.-focused, dated tax information.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A 35-year-old wants a house deposit in two years and to retire in 30 years. Which plan best applies the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Separate the goals: liquid, low-volatility short-term investments for the deposit, and a long-term, growth-oriented portfolio for retirement."], ["B", "Keep everything in cash until the house is bought, then start investing."], ["C", "One aggressive portfolio for both goals, since retirement is the longer goal."], ["D", "Put both goals into whichever asset had the highest return last year."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>One investor can have several goals with different horizons, liquidity needs and capacities for loss, so each needs a matching strategy. C risks the deposit; B wastes the long retirement horizon; D chases past returns.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Design the order of an investment plan for a new graduate with RM15,000 savings, unstable freelance income and no insurance, who wants to \"start investing in stocks\".</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy stocks now, then build an emergency fund from the profits."], ["B", "Build an emergency fund and basic protection first, set specific goals and target dates, then evaluate return and risk before selecting a diversified portfolio, and monitor it."], ["C", "Choose the stock with the best recent return, then decide the goal."], ["D", "Put everything in a 10-year fixed deposit."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This follows the seven steps in order: prerequisites → goals → plan → evaluate → select → diversify → manage. Unstable income makes the emergency fund especially important.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A retiree depends partly on portfolio withdrawals. Which combination of objectives should a portfolio for them emphasise, per the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Preservation of capital, a conservative portfolio and investment income."], ["B", "Capital gains and maximum growth."], ["C", "Options and futures for higher returns."], ["D", "Only foreign equities for diversification."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In the retirement stage the slides stress capital preservation, a highly conservative portfolio and income to supplement retirement income, because losses have little time to recover and current income is needed.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design a single question that would separate a student who memorised the seven steps from one who understands them.</p>", "options": [["A", "\"List the seven steps in order.\""], ["B", "\"What is step 4?\""], ["C", "\"A client with no emergency fund wants to buy shares tomorrow because a friend recommended them; explain which steps are being skipped, why the order matters, and what you would do first.\""], ["D", "\"Define investment.\""]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Recall questions (A, B, D) can be answered by memorisation. C requires applying and evaluating the logic of the process, the Bloom level the chapter wants for subjective answers.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement defines the <strong>secondary market</strong> correctly?</p>", "options": [["A", "The market where short-term securities are traded."], ["B", "The market in which securities are traded after they have been issued."], ["C", "The market where new issues are sold to the public."], ["D", "The market for foreign securities."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Secondary = trading after issuance. A is the money market; C is the primary market; D is not a separate category in the chapter.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is a <strong>red herring</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "The final prospectus filed after the offering."], ["B", "A preliminary prospectus made available during the waiting period."], ["C", "A public advertisement announcing a completed issue."], ["D", "An illegal promotion of a stock."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The red herring is the preliminary prospectus. The public announcement of an issue and its underwriters is the <strong>tombstone</strong> (C).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which law established the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)?</p>", "options": [["A", "Securities Exchange Act of 1934"], ["B", "Securities Act of 1933"], ["C", "Maloney Act of 1938"], ["D", "Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>1933 required full disclosure for new issues; 1934 created the SEC; 1938 allowed industry self-regulation (NASD); 2002 tightened accounting and audit rules after corporate fraud.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why are secondary markets economically important even though issuers receive no money from resales?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because they set the coupon rate on new bonds."], ["B", "Because they eliminate investment risk."], ["C", "Because issuers receive a commission on each resale."], ["D", "Because they provide liquidity and continuous pricing, which make buying new issues more attractive."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides list liquidity and continuous pricing as the secondary market's role. An exit route and a market price make investors more willing to buy in the primary market.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In an underwritten offering, what does the investment bank mainly take on?</p>", "options": [["A", "The risk that the issuer's profits fall in future years."], ["B", "The legal liability for the issuer's debts."], ["C", "The risk of reselling the issue to the public at a price above what it paid."], ["D", "The risk of currency movements."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Underwriting = the bank buys the issue at an agreed price and bears the risk of reselling it. It is compensated through the discount (spread) on the offering price.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does a margin investor's <strong>percentage</strong> gain or loss exceed the stock's percentage move?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the broker adds a bonus to margin accounts."], ["B", "Because the stock price is quoted on margin."], ["C", "Because margin accounts pay higher dividends."], ["D", "Because the gain or loss is measured on the investor's equity, which is smaller than the position, while the loan stays fixed."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The same ringgit gain or loss is divided by a smaller equity base. The loan does not change with the price, so equity absorbs the full move.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Why do dealers widen their bid-ask spreads in volatile, thinly traded stocks?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their inventory risk and the difficulty of finding the other side are greater, so they need more compensation for providing liquidity."], ["B", "Regulators require it."], ["C", "Volatile stocks pay higher dividends."], ["D", "Spreads are fixed by the exchange."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The spread compensates dealers for liquidity provision and inventory risk. More risk and less trading means a wider spread, a higher transaction cost for investors.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A dealer quotes a share at RM4.85 bid / RM4.90 ask. You sell 2,000 shares. How much do you receive, ignoring commissions?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM9,800"], ["B", "RM9,700"], ["C", "RM9,750"], ["D", "RM9,900"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>You sell to the dealer at the <strong>bid</strong>: 2,000 x 4.85 = RM9,700. Buying would cost the ask: 2,000 x 4.90 = RM9,800 (A).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You buy 200 shares at RM40 using 60% initial margin. The price later rises to RM46. Ignoring interest and dividends, what is your return on invested equity?</p>", "options": [["A", "15.0%"], ["B", "9.0%"], ["C", "25.0%"], ["D", "37.5%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Position = RM8,000; equity = 0.60 x 8,000 = RM4,800. Gain = 200 x 6 = RM1,200. Return on equity = 1,200 / 4,800 = <strong>25%</strong>. The unlevered return is 15% (A).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You short 300 shares at RM25 and cover at RM31. Ignoring costs, what is the result?</p>", "options": [["A", "Profit of RM1,800"], ["B", "Profit of RM9,300"], ["C", "Loss of RM7,500"], ["D", "Loss of RM1,800"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Short profit = (sale price - buy-back price) x shares = (25 - 31) x 300 = <strong>-RM1,800</strong>. A rising price hurts the short seller.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A broker's maintenance margin is 30%. A client's long margin position is worth RM50,000 with a RM40,000 loan. How much must the client deposit (as cash that reduces the loan) to restore 30%?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM15,000"], ["B", "RM5,000"], ["C", "RM10,000"], ["D", "Nothing"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current margin = 10,000 / 50,000 = 20%. Needed: (50,000 - D) / 50,000 = 0.30, so D = 35,000. Paying RM5,000 off the loan restores 30%.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>An investor buys 1,000 shares at RM20 with 50% initial margin, pays RM600 interest, receives RM400 dividends, and sells at RM23. What is the return on invested capital?</p>", "options": [["A", "15.0%"], ["B", "30.0%"], ["C", "28.0%"], ["D", "34.0%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Return = (income - interest + sale value - purchase value) / initial equity = (400 - 600 + 23,000 - 20,000) / 10,000 = 2,800 / 10,000 = <strong>28%</strong>. Without interest it would be 34% (D).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A margin account holds shares worth RM12,000 with a loan of RM6,000. The price falls 25%. What happens to the margin?</p>", "options": [["A", "It stays at 50% because the loan is unchanged."], ["B", "It falls to 25%."], ["C", "It rises to 66.7%."], ["D", "It falls to 33.3%: equity drops to RM3,000 on a value of RM9,000."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>New V = 9,000, D = 6,000, equity = 3,000. Margin = 3,000 / 9,000 = <strong>33.3%</strong>. The stock fell 25%, but equity fell 50% (from 6,000 to 3,000).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You buy 100 shares at RM60 with a RM3,000 loan. The maintenance margin is 25%. Below roughly what share price is a margin call triggered?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM30.00"], ["B", "RM40.00"], ["C", "RM45.00"], ["D", "RM22.50"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$V^* = D / (1 - m) = 3{,}000 / 0.75 = 4{,}000$; per share 4,000 / 100 = <strong>RM40</strong>. Check: at RM40, equity = 1,000 and margin = 1,000 / 4,000 = 25%.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two pension funds trade a large block of shares directly with each other through an electronic network, with no market maker. Which market is this?</p>", "options": [["A", "Primary market"], ["B", "Fourth market"], ["C", "Third market"], ["D", "Over-the-counter dealer market"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The fourth market is institutions trading directly with each other, often via ECNs, bypassing market makers. In the third market, a non-member market maker is involved.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>In a short sale (Table 2.6 logic), the investor sold 100 shares at RM50 and deposited RM2,500. At what share price does short-account margin fall to 30%?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM55.00"], ["B", "RM75.00"], ["C", "RM65.00"], ["D", "RM57.69"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Funds held = 7,500. Margin = (7,500 - 100P) / (100P) = 0.30 → 7,500 = 130P → P ≈ <strong>RM57.69</strong>.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A popular stock is heavily shorted, then jumps on good news, and short sellers rush to buy shares to close their positions. What is the likely effect?</p>", "options": [["A", "The price falls, because short sellers are selling."], ["B", "No effect, because shorts are closed off-exchange."], ["C", "Their buying adds demand and can push the price up further: a \"short squeeze\" that magnifies short sellers' losses."], ["D", "Margin calls only affect long investors."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Closing a short requires buying. Forced buying from rising losses and margin calls adds demand, an extension of the chapter's point that a rising price hurts short sellers, and can feed on itself.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why might IPO shares often be priced so that they rise on the first trading day?</p>", "options": [["A", "Underwriters always overprice."], ["B", "First-day returns are guaranteed by the issuer."], ["C", "Regulators fix first-day returns."], ["D", "Leaving some \"money on the table\" can compensate investors for information asymmetry and help underwriters place a large, risky issue fully."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's Table 2.1 shows positive average first-day returns. A common explanation builds on its information-asymmetry and underwriting-risk logic: pricing slightly low attracts investors and reduces the risk of an unsold issue.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Leverage of 2x (50% margin) doubles the expected excess return on equity. What does it do to the standard deviation of equity returns, ignoring interest-rate changes?</p>", "options": [["A", "Roughly doubles it, so the risk-return trade-off per unit of risk is not improved."], ["B", "Leaves it unchanged."], ["C", "Halves it."], ["D", "Removes it."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Scaling a position by 2 scales both its mean excess return and its volatility by about 2. Leverage magnifies; it does not create a better risk-return ratio, and borrowing costs make it slightly worse.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Short selling and buying on margin carry the same risk because both involve borrowing.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incorrect, because shorting involves no borrowing."], ["C", "Incorrect: margin borrows money and profits when prices rise, its loss limited by the price reaching zero; shorting borrows shares and profits when prices fall, with a theoretically unlimited loss as prices rise."], ["D", "Correct, because both require a broker."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both create obligations, but different ones (owe money vs owe shares) with different payoff shapes. The unlimited-loss asymmetry of the short sale is the key point.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A U.S. stock gains 12% in dollars while the dollar falls 10% against the ringgit. Evaluate the Malaysian investor's claim: \"I made 12%.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Wrong: the ringgit return is (1.12)(0.90) - 1 ≈ 0.8%."], ["B", "Correct; currency doesn't matter."], ["C", "Wrong: the ringgit return is 22%."], ["D", "Wrong: the ringgit return is -10%."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Home-currency return compounds the asset return and the currency change: 1.12 x 0.90 = 1.008, about <strong>+0.8%</strong>. Foreign asset return ≠ domestic investor return.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A friend says: \"I'm certain this stock will rise, so I'll use the maximum margin available.\" What is the strongest evaluation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Leverage raises the sensitivity of equity to forecast errors, adds interest cost and margin-call risk: a correct long-term view can still end in a forced sale after a short-term fall."], ["B", "Sensible, because confidence reduces risk."], ["C", "Sensible, because margin calls only happen in bear markets."], ["D", "Wrong only because margin interest is tax-deductible."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Certainty about direction does not remove uncertainty about the path. Margin turns a temporary fall into a possible permanent loss if the investor is forced out.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A start-up chooses a private placement instead of an IPO. Which trade-off is most relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is faster and less costly in disclosure and marketing, but reaches fewer investors and gives them a less liquid security."], ["B", "A private placement always raises more money."], ["C", "It removes all regulation."], ["D", "It makes the shares trade on an exchange immediately."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The three primary-market methods differ in who can buy and how. A placement sells to selected investors: less public disclosure and distribution cost, but a narrower investor base and less liquidity.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Insider trading rules only protect the people on the other side of insiders' trades.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Correct, because markets are always efficient."], ["C", "Incorrect, because insider trading is legal."], ["D", "Incomplete: they also protect the market itself, because if investors believe trading is rigged, participation, liquidity and the cost of capital for all firms suffer."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter frames regulation as preserving confidence in the market mechanism, a benefit that reaches every issuer and investor.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Some economists argue short selling helps markets. Which argument is most consistent with the chapter's view of secondary markets?</p>", "options": [["A", "Short sellers guarantee prices never fall."], ["B", "Short selling raises companies' new capital."], ["C", "Allowing pessimistic investors to trade lets negative information enter prices, improving continuous pricing (price discovery)."], ["D", "Short sellers pay dividends to issuers."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Secondary markets provide continuous pricing. If only optimists can act, prices may reflect one side of the information. This extends the chapter's price-discovery idea.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>The chapter's market statistics (NYSE share of trading, trading hours, laws) date from 2014. What is the most rigorous way to use them?</p>", "options": [["A", "Use them as the course's reference values while recognising that market structures, regulations and venues evolve; the durable lessons are the functions (liquidity, price discovery, disclosure)."], ["B", "Treat them as current facts."], ["C", "Ignore the chapter."], ["D", "Assume Bursa Malaysia works identically."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes stress separating course material from current market facts. The economic functions are stable; the institutional details are not.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company needs RM800 million quickly and fears weak demand for its first share issue. Which arrangement best addresses the risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sell the shares itself through its website."], ["B", "Issue a red herring and skip the road show."], ["C", "Use an investment bank to underwrite the issue, with a syndicate to share the risk and a selling group to widen distribution."], ["D", "List directly on the fourth market."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Underwriting transfers the resale risk to the bank; a syndicate spreads a large exposure; a selling group reaches more investors. The road show (dropped in B) helps gauge demand and set the price.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor expects a stock to fall but wants to cap the loss if it instead rises sharply. Which combination fits the chapter's tools?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy the stock on margin with a sell stop below the price."], ["B", "Short the stock and place a buy stop above the current price."], ["C", "Short the stock with no protective order."], ["D", "Buy the stock with cash and a limit order."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The short position expresses the bearish view; a buy stop above the price triggers a purchase to close the short if the price rises, limiting the otherwise unlimited loss (a Chapter 3 order type).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You are designing a lesson to show why the <strong>secondary</strong> market matters to a firm that never sells shares there. Which example makes the point most directly?</p>", "options": [["A", "A firm pays dividends."], ["B", "Investors pay more for a firm's IPO shares when they know they can resell them easily at a visible market price later."], ["C", "A firm issues commercial paper."], ["D", "A firm's bonds have fixed coupons."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It links secondary-market liquidity and pricing directly to the terms the firm gets in the primary market: the indirect economic value of resale markets.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design a single scenario that tests whether a student understands \"market direction + position structure = investor outcome\".</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Define a short sale.\""], ["B", "\"What year was the SEC created?\""], ["C", "\"List three exchanges.\""], ["D", "\"A stock rises 20%. Compute the returns of a cash buyer, a 50%-margin buyer and a short seller, and explain why they differ.\""]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The same price move produces +20%, about +40% and a loss, forcing the student to apply the mechanics of all three positions: analysis rather than recall.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which kind of information reports <strong>factual data on the past behaviour</strong> of the economy, a market, an industry or a company?</p>", "options": [["A", "Analytical information"], ["B", "Speculative information"], ["C", "Descriptive information"], ["D", "Insider information"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Descriptive information is factual data on past behaviour. Analytical information combines current data with projections and recommendations about potential investments.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What does Regulation FD require?</p>", "options": [["A", "That companies pay dividends every year."], ["B", "That brokers register with the SEC."], ["C", "That critical company information be disclosed to investment professionals and the public at the same time."], ["D", "That all trades be reported to an exchange within a day."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Regulation FD (fair disclosure) prevents selective disclosure of material information to a privileged group. Broker registration (B) is a different requirement.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which description of a <strong>limit order</strong> matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "An order to buy at or below a specified price, or to sell at or above a specified price; if the limit is not met, the order is not filled."], ["B", "An order to buy or sell at the best price available when it is placed."], ["C", "An order that becomes a market order once a trigger price is reached."], ["D", "An order that expires at the end of the day."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>B is a market order, C is a stop (stop-loss) order and D describes a day order, which is a time condition rather than a price condition.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the chapter warn that online investing removes a \"safety net\"?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because there is no live broker to question or slow down a decision, so mistakes and impulsive trades are easier to make."], ["B", "Because online brokers are always unregulated."], ["C", "Because online trades are slower."], ["D", "Because online investing prohibits limit orders."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The convenience of acting instantly also removes useful friction: a conversation with a broker. The chapter pairs this with warnings about \"free\" advice, frequent trading and margin.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why is the S&amp;P 500 considered more representative of the overall U.S. market than the DJIA?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is calculated using a different calculator."], ["B", "It includes 500 companies from major industries, versus the DJIA's 30 stocks."], ["C", "It contains only technology stocks."], ["D", "It is published more often."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Breadth is the point: more companies across more industries. The DJIA's 30 high-quality stocks make it popular but narrower.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is <strong>street name</strong> registration?</p>", "options": [["A", "Registering securities in the investor's home address."], ["B", "Registering securities in the brokerage firm's name while they are held in trust for the client, who remains the beneficial owner."], ["C", "A way to hide ownership from regulators."], ["D", "Ownership transferred permanently to the broker."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The broker is the registered holder for convenience, but the investor keeps the economic ownership. Registration convenience is not economic ownership.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Why might an equal-weighted index and a market-value-weighted index of the same stocks give different returns in the same year?</p>", "options": [["A", "They use different stocks."], ["B", "Value-weighted indexes ignore market values."], ["C", "Equal-weighted indexes ignore prices."], ["D", "In a value-weighted index the largest companies dominate; in an equal-weighted index every stock counts the same, so a rise in small stocks affects the two differently."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Weighting method determines which stocks drive the index. An index is not neutral: its construction methodology affects what it measures.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A stock trades near RM30. You want to buy but refuse to pay more than RM29.50. Which order fits?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market order"], ["B", "Sell stop order at RM29.50"], ["C", "Limit buy order at RM29.50"], ["D", "Fill-or-kill market order"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A limit order protects the price: it executes only at RM29.50 or lower. A market order prioritises execution and could fill above RM29.50. A sell stop is for selling.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor buys 1,000 shares at RM5.00 and sells at RM5.30. Commissions are RM30 on each trade. What is the net profit?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM300"], ["B", "RM240"], ["C", "RM270"], ["D", "RM210"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Gross profit = 1,000 x 0.30 = RM300. Total commissions = 2 x 30 = RM60. Net = 300 - 60 = <strong>RM240</strong>.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You own a stock bought at RM20, now RM25, and place a sell stop at RM22. Overnight bad news makes the stock open at RM18. What most likely happens?</p>", "options": [["A", "The order sells at exactly RM22."], ["B", "The order becomes a buy order."], ["C", "The order is cancelled and no sale occurs."], ["D", "The stop triggers and becomes a market order, so the sale executes near RM18, below the stop price."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed price. Once triggered the order becomes a market order and fills at the best available price, which here is far lower.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A price-weighted average has three stocks priced RM10, RM20 and RM90. Which stock dominates, and what happens if the RM90 stock rises 10% while the others are unchanged?</p>", "options": [["A", "The RM10 stock dominates; the average rises about 15%."], ["B", "All stocks are equal; the average is unchanged."], ["C", "The RM90 stock dominates; the average rises from 40 to 43, about 7.5%."], ["D", "The RM20 stock dominates; the average rises 1%."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A price-weighted average is the average of prices: (10 + 20 + 90) / 3 = 40. After the rise: (10 + 20 + 99) / 3 = 43, a 7.5% increase. The highest-priced stock has the biggest influence.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A bond with a face value of 1,000 and a 6% coupon trades at 950. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The current yield equals 6% because the coupon is fixed."], ["B", "The yield is below 6%."], ["C", "The yield to maturity is above 6%, because the buyer receives the coupon plus a price gain to par at maturity."], ["D", "Yield cannot be compared with the coupon rate."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes bond yield is total return from buying at the current price and holding to maturity: coupon plus the gain from 950 to 1,000. Coupon ≠ yield.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why can a stop-<strong>limit</strong> order fail to protect an investor on a sharp fall?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because it becomes a market order."], ["B", "Because it becomes a limit order once triggered, and if the price falls through the limit without trading at that level, the order may not fill at all."], ["C", "Because it can only be used for buying."], ["D", "Because it executes before the stop price."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A stop-limit gives price control but gives up execution certainty. A plain stop gives execution at an uncertain price.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A broker earns a commission on every trade and recommends that a retiree \"rotate\" their portfolio monthly. What risk is most relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "Churning: the broker's incentive (more trades, more commissions) may not align with the client's interest"], ["B", "Currency risk"], ["C", "Event risk"], ["D", "Reinvestment risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Excessive trading to generate commissions is churning. Transaction costs are a hurdle every trade must clear, so frequent trading usually reduces net returns.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A balanced fund of Malaysian small-cap stocks returned 12% while the S&amp;P 500 returned 18%. Which analysis is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund underperformed by 6 percentage points."], ["B", "The comparison is unreliable: the S&amp;P 500 does not represent the fund's investment universe, so a small-cap benchmark should be used."], ["C", "The fund beat the market, because small caps are riskier."], ["D", "Benchmarks are irrelevant."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Benchmark selection must match the exposure being evaluated. Comparing a Malaysian small-cap portfolio with the S&amp;P 500 is not automatically meaningful.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>An investor places a market order to sell 10,000 shares of a thinly traded stock. Which outcome is the main risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Poor execution: the order is large relative to market depth, so successive fills may occur at progressively worse prices."], ["B", "The order will never execute."], ["C", "The order becomes a limit order."], ["D", "The order expires at day end."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A market order prioritises execution, not price. In a thin market, a large order moves the price against the seller; a limit order would control price at the cost of fill certainty.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A forum user posts a strong buy case and mentions owning the stock. Which is the best first step?</p>", "options": [["A", "Treat the post as opinion from an interested party, verify its claims against filings and primary evidence, and ask what the poster gains if readers buy."], ["B", "Buy because the post is detailed."], ["C", "Ignore all online information."], ["D", "Sell the stock short immediately."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Source incentives matter (pump-and-dump). The skill is to separate evidence from narrative, not to dismiss information.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why can two broad indexes with similar long-run trends still differ materially over a two-year window, as in Figure 3.5?</p>", "options": [["A", "One of them is wrong."], ["B", "Indexes differ only in how often they are published."], ["C", "They hold different securities and weights, so shocks to particular sectors or large companies affect them by different amounts."], ["D", "One excludes dividends by law."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Same market environment ≠ identical index return. Composition and weighting create the divergence.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Behavioural finance suggests investors trade more after gains. Which chapter idea connects most directly?</p>", "options": [["A", "Mediation versus arbitration"], ["B", "The definition of the S&amp;P 500"], ["C", "The warning about frequent trading and day trading: more trades raise costs and error opportunities without necessarily improving returns."], ["D", "Street name registration"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Activity feels like control but costs money. The chapter's stance is that more trades do not automatically mean more return.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"A stop-loss order guarantees that my maximum loss is the difference between my purchase price and the stop price.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incorrect: the stop becomes a market order when triggered, so a gap can fill well below the stop; a stop-limit may not fill at all."], ["C", "Correct, but only for day orders."], ["D", "Incorrect, because stops do not exist for long positions."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Stop price = trigger, not guaranteed sale price. This is among the chapter's most testable ideas.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"SIPC protection means my investments cannot lose money if my broker fails.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incorrect: SIPC protects against certain losses from a broker's financial failure, but not against market losses, bad advice or churning."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct, because SIPC insures market value without limit."], ["D", "Incorrect, because SIPC only protects the broker."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Investor protection means protection against certain institutional failures, not against ordinary investment losses. The slides cite historical limits (USD 500,000 for securities, USD 250,000 for cash).</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Advisors are registered with the SEC, so their advice is reliable.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incorrect: registration means meeting regulatory requirements; the slides note that no law or regulator guarantees competence, so experience and credentials still matter."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct, because registration tests investment skill."], ["D", "Incorrect, because advisors cannot register."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Licensed does not imply good. A good advisor also asks many questions about the client's goals and circumstances before recommending anything.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A brokerage offers zero commissions but earns money by routing orders to market makers. Evaluate \"free trading means no trading costs\".</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incomplete: an explicit commission is only one cost; spreads and the execution price also matter, and zero commission may encourage more frequent trading."], ["C", "Incorrect, because market makers do not exist."], ["D", "Correct, because spreads are zero."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Transaction costs include commissions, bid-ask spreads and poor execution. Lower visible friction can raise trading frequency, the behavioural issue the chapter flags.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"A good advisor who charges 1% of assets a year is worth it if performance matches the market.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because matching the market is the goal."], ["B", "Correct, because fees are never relevant."], ["C", "Doubtful on returns alone: the fee reduces net return relative to the benchmark unless the advisor adds value through planning, discipline, tax and risk management that the investor could not obtain cheaply."], ["D", "Incorrect, because advisors cannot match the market."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes advice fees and that advice can improve process, not eliminate risk. Value must be weighed against net-of-fee results and services received.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An analyst argues that because Regulation FD forces simultaneous disclosure, stock prices fully reflect all information instantly. Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Fully correct."], ["B", "Correct because analysts do not exist."], ["C", "Wrong because FD allows selective disclosure."], ["D", "Overreach: simultaneous disclosure improves fairness of access, but interpreting information still takes judgment and resources, so prices may adjust unevenly."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Equal access to information is not equal ability to interpret it. The chapter's descriptive-versus-analytical distinction supports this.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>The chapter's brokerage examples, SIPC limits and index lists are from 2014. What is the best research practice?</p>", "options": [["A", "Quote them as current rules."], ["B", "Assume they never change."], ["C", "Delete all numbers."], ["D", "Use them as the course's reference points and verify current specifics from primary sources before applying them."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Institutional details change; the economic functions (execution, protection, benchmarking) are durable.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Design a sound process for a student wanting to invest in a technology company after seeing an online recommendation.</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy immediately before the price moves."], ["B", "Copy a friend's portfolio."], ["C", "Ask an anonymous forum for a price target and buy the next day."], ["D", "Read the company's filings, review its financial performance, separate facts from forecasts, compare with competitors and a relevant benchmark, set an entry price, place a limit order and monitor the position."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It uses primary sources, separates descriptive from analytical information, uses a benchmark and chooses an order type that matches the price condition.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor sees a stock at RM28 but is only willing to buy at RM25 or below, and is happy to wait several weeks. Which order set-up fits best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market order, day"], ["B", "Fill-or-kill market order"], ["C", "Sell stop at RM25"], ["D", "Limit buy at RM25, good-'til-canceled"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The price condition is \"RM25 or lower\" (limit) and the time condition is \"willing to wait\" (GTC). A market order would buy at RM28.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which design would best protect an investor who has shorted a stock at RM50 against a sharp rise?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy stop at RM60"], ["B", "Sell stop at RM40"], ["C", "Limit sell at RM60"], ["D", "No order"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A buy stop above the current price is activated if the price rises to RM60, triggering a purchase that closes the short. The slides note stops can be used to buy stock to limit risk on short positions.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which exam question best distinguishes a student who understands order types from one who memorised definitions?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Define a market order.\""], ["B", "\"What does GTC stand for?\""], ["C", "\"List four order types.\""], ["D", "\"A holder of a stock bought at RM20 (now RM25) wants downside protection and also wants to avoid selling in a panic gap. Compare a stop and a stop-limit order and recommend one, justifying it.\""]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It requires applying the execution-certainty versus price-control trade-off to a specific objective, the Bloom Apply-Evaluate level.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement defines the time value of money?</p>", "options": [["A", "Money loses value only when prices rise."], ["B", "As long as money can earn interest, its value depends on the point in time at which it is received."], ["C", "Interest is paid only on the original deposit."], ["D", "Cash flows in the future are always larger than cash flows today."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A dollar (or ringgit) received sooner can start earning interest sooner, so it is worth more than the same amount later. A describes inflation, C simple interest, and D is not true in general.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which equation gives the <strong>required return</strong> in the chapter's model?</p>", "options": [["A", "Required return = real rate x inflation premium x risk premium"], ["B", "Required return = real rate + expected inflation premium + risk premium"], ["C", "Required return = expected return - risk premium"], ["D", "Required return = risk-free rate - risk premium"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The three components add. Because the risk-free rate equals real rate plus inflation premium, this simplifies to R<sub>f</sub> + risk premium.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What does the <strong>coefficient of variation</strong> measure?</p>", "options": [["A", "Absolute dispersion of returns"], ["B", "The sensitivity of a stock to the market"], ["C", "The slope of the security market line"], ["D", "Relative dispersion: standard deviation per unit of average return"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CV = σ / mean return. Standard deviation is the absolute measure (A); beta and the SML belong to Chapter 5.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the present value of a future payment fall when the discount rate rises?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the payment itself shrinks."], ["B", "Because inflation rises with the discount rate."], ["C", "Because a higher opportunity rate means less money today is needed to grow into the same future amount."], ["D", "Because compounding becomes simple interest."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV = FV / (1 + r)<sup>n</sup>. A larger r makes the denominator larger. Economically, if you can earn more elsewhere, you need to put less aside now.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why can holding-period return (HPR) mislead when the holding period exceeds a year?</p>", "options": [["A", "HPR does not account for the time value of money, so returns over different horizons are not comparable."], ["B", "HPR ignores capital gains."], ["C", "HPR only works for bonds."], ["D", "HPR cannot be negative."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A 40% return over one year and a 40% return over five years have the same HPR but very different annual performance. Compound annual yield (IRR) fixes this.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which pair correctly contrasts business risk and financial risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business risk comes from interest rates; financial risk comes from inflation."], ["B", "Business risk affects bonds only; financial risk affects stocks only."], ["C", "Business risk concerns uncertainty in the firm's earnings and operations; financial risk concerns uncertainty from its debt-and-equity financing mix."], ["D", "They are the same risk described differently."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Two identical restaurants share similar business risk, but the one with heavy debt carries more financial risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A retiree holds a bond paying a fixed RM5,000 a year while inflation rises sharply. Which risk is most directly at work?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business risk"], ["B", "Purchasing-power risk"], ["C", "Event risk"], ["D", "Financial risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The nominal payment is unchanged, but its real value falls. Fixed-income securities and deposits are the chapter's examples of purchasing-power risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>RM5,000 is deposited for 4 years at 6% compounded annually. What is the future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM6,200.00"], ["B", "RM6,312.38"], ["C", "RM6,000.00"], ["D", "RM6,524.77"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>FV = 5,000 x (1.06)<sup>4</sup> = 5,000 x 1.262477 = <strong>RM6,312.38</strong>. A uses simple interest-like growth; C is simple interest at 5%; D uses 7%.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is the present value of RM10,000 due in 3 years at an 8% discount rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM7,938.32"], ["B", "RM7,500.00"], ["C", "RM8,000.00"], ["D", "RM8,264.46"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV = 10,000 / 1.08<sup>3</sup> = 10,000 / 1.259712 = <strong>RM7,938.32</strong>. D discounts for only 2 years.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A share is bought at RM80, pays a RM4 dividend, and is worth RM84 at year-end. What is the HPR?</p>", "options": [["A", "5%"], ["B", "10%"], ["C", "15%"], ["D", "4%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>HPR = [income + (P<sub>1</sub> - P<sub>0</sub>)] / P<sub>0</sub> = (4 + 4) / 80 = <strong>10%</strong>. A counts only the dividend or only the gain.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A company borrows RM100,000 at 8% and repays it in 5 equal year-end instalments. Approximately what is each instalment?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM25,046"], ["B", "RM20,000"], ["C", "RM28,000"], ["D", "RM21,600"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The loan is the present value of an ordinary annuity: PMT = 100,000 / [(1 - 1.08<sup>-5</sup>) / 0.08] = 100,000 / 3.99271 ≈ <strong>RM25,046</strong>.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A stated rate of 8% is compounded quarterly. What is the effective annual rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "8.00%"], ["B", "8.50%"], ["C", "8.24%"], ["D", "8.32%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>(1 + 0.08/4)<sup>4</sup> - 1 = 1.02<sup>4</sup> - 1 = 1.082432 - 1 ≈ <strong>8.24%</strong>. More frequent compounding raises the true rate.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>RM1,000 is deposited at the end of each year for 8 years at 8% (future value RM10,636.63). Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Interest earned is RM2,636.63, because earlier deposits compound for longer."], ["B", "Interest earned is RM8,000."], ["C", "The future value equals RM8,000 x 1.08."], ["D", "The last deposit earns seven years of interest."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Total deposits = RM8,000, so interest = 10,636.63 - 8,000 = <strong>RM2,636.63</strong>. The first deposit compounds seven years and the last (made at the valuation date) earns nothing.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Investment A has a mean return of 10% and σ of 20%. Investment B has a mean of 20% and σ of 30%. Which is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is riskier on both measures."], ["B", "A has lower σ, but B has lower relative dispersion (CV 1.5 versus 2.0)."], ["C", "B has lower σ and lower CV."], ["D", "CV cannot be computed for these data."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CV(A) = 20 / 10 = 2.0; CV(B) = 30 / 20 = 1.5. Absolute and relative risk can rank investments differently.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An asset is bought for RM1,000 and sold for RM1,400 five years later, with no income. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its compound annual return is 8%."], ["B", "Its compound annual return is 40%."], ["C", "Its HPR is 40%, and its compound annual return is about 6.96%."], ["D", "Its HPR is 6.96%."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>HPR = 400 / 1,000 = 40%. Annual yield = 1.4<sup>1/5</sup> - 1 ≈ 6.96%. Dividing 40% by 5 (= 8%) ignores compounding.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>At 9% compounded annually, roughly how long does money take to double?</p>", "options": [["A", "About 4 years"], ["B", "About 16 years"], ["C", "About 12 years"], ["D", "About 8 years"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Solve 1.09<sup>n</sup> = 2: n = ln 2 / ln 1.09 ≈ 8.04 years. (The \"rule of 72\" gives 72 / 9 = 8.)</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>An investment costs RM12,000 and pays RM4,000, RM5,000 and RM6,000 at the ends of years 1-3. At a 10% required return its NPV is about +RM276. What happens at a 12% required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "NPV rises, because the payments are unchanged."], ["B", "NPV stays exactly the same."], ["C", "NPV turns negative (PV of benefits ≈ RM11,828 &lt; RM12,000), reversing the decision."], ["D", "The investment becomes risk-free."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>At 12%: 4,000/1.12 + 5,000/1.12<sup>2</sup> + 6,000/1.12<sup>3</sup> ≈ 3,571 + 3,986 + 4,271 = 11,828. A thin positive NPV has little room for adverse changes in assumptions.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why does using IRR to evaluate a bond implicitly assume reinvestment of coupons at the IRR itself?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the IRR solves for one single rate that discounts every cash flow, which is equivalent to compounding each interim cash flow forward at that same rate."], ["B", "Because coupons are always spent."], ["C", "Because IRR ignores coupons."], ["D", "Because reinvestment rates are fixed by law."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter flags this assumption: if actual reinvestment rates are lower, the fully compounded return falls below the IRR.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An investment returns +50% in year 1 and -50% in year 2. The arithmetic average return is 0%. What happens to wealth?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is unchanged."], ["B", "It rises by 25%."], ["C", "It falls to 75% of the starting amount, a compound annual return of about -13.4%."], ["D", "It falls to 50% of the starting amount."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>1.5 x 0.5 = 0.75. The compound (geometric) annual return is √0.75 - 1 ≈ -13.4%. This is why averages of annual returns overstate compound growth when returns are volatile.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"An investment expected to return 12% is attractive, because 12% is a good return.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incomplete: it depends on the required return for its risk; if the required return is 14%, 12% is inadequate."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct if the investment is a stock."], ["D", "Incorrect, because only returns above 20% are attractive."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Expected return is a forecast; required return is the hurdle. No return is good or bad in isolation.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Investment B has the lower standard deviation, so it is the better investment.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incomplete: low risk is not automatically superior if the return is far lower; the decision depends on required return, risk preference and objectives."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Incorrect, because σ is irrelevant."], ["D", "Correct if B is a bond."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter teaches optimising the relationship between return and risk, not minimising risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Bank A offers 12.5% compounded annually. Bank B offers 12% compounded monthly. Which is better?</p>", "options": [["A", "A, since 12.5% &gt; 12%."], ["B", "B, since monthly compounding always beats annual compounding."], ["C", "They are equal."], ["D", "B: its effective annual rate is (1.01)<sup>12</sup> - 1 ≈ 12.68%, above A's 12.5%."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Compare effective annual rates, not stated rates. Option D overgeneralises: monthly compounding beats annual compounding only at the same stated rate; here the stated rates differ, so the calculation decides.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>Fund A gained 50% over 4 years. Fund B earned 11% a year for 4 years. Which is better on a compound annual basis?</p>", "options": [["A", "B: A's compound annual return is 1.5<sup>1/4</sup> - 1 ≈ 10.67%, below B's 11%."], ["B", "A, because 50% is larger than 11%."], ["C", "They are equal."], ["D", "A, because it earned its return faster."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Always annualise before comparing horizons.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"A 10-year historical standard deviation tells us exactly how volatile the investment will be next year.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Overstated: it is an estimate from a limited sample that can change; it is evidence for forming expectations, not a promise."], ["C", "Correct for stocks but not for bonds."], ["D", "Incorrect because standard deviation is always zero."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter says historical performance provides a basis for expectations but not a guarantee. A five-observation sample, as in the Target example, offers limited evidence.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>When is the coefficient of variation a poor measure?</p>", "options": [["A", "When returns are always positive."], ["B", "When the mean return is near zero or negative, so the ratio becomes huge, undefined or hard to interpret."], ["C", "When there are more than 10 observations."], ["D", "When returns are in ringgit."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CV = σ / mean. A mean near zero makes CV explode; a negative mean makes its sign meaningless. Use σ and judgement in such cases.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Textbooks use the 3-month Treasury bill as the risk-free rate. When discounting a 25-year project, what is the main conceptual concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "T-bills pay no interest."], ["B", "No concern: the risk-free rate never changes."], ["C", "T-bills are riskier than stocks."], ["D", "A maturity mismatch: a short-term rate reflects short-term conditions, so the discount rate for long cash flows should reflect longer-horizon rates and the project's own risk."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The risk-free rate is a proxy. Required return also needs a risk premium appropriate to the project and the horizon.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You need RM50,000 in 5 years and can earn 8% a year. Which equal year-end contribution is required (approximately)?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM10,000"], ["B", "RM6,250"], ["C", "RM7,500"], ["D", "RM8,523"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>FV annuity factor = (1.08<sup>5</sup> - 1) / 0.08 = 5.8666. PMT = 50,000 / 5.8666 ≈ <strong>RM8,523</strong>. A ignores interest.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which sequence best builds a decision framework for choosing among three investments, per the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose the highest expected return."], ["B", "Choose the lowest standard deviation."], ["C", "Estimate expected returns; determine the required return for each risk; measure risk with SD and CV; compare expected with required return; choose the one consistent with the investor's risk preference."], ["D", "Choose the one with the longest history."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is the chapter's four-step decision process, combining return, risk and investor preference.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor estimates a real return of 2.5%, expected inflation of 3% and a risk premium of 4% for a particular security. What required return should the investor use as the discount rate or hurdle?</p>", "options": [["A", "5.5%"], ["B", "7.5%"], ["C", "4.0%"], ["D", "9.5%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>R<sub>f</sub> = 2.5% + 3% = 5.5%; required return = 5.5% + 4% = <strong>9.5%</strong>. A is the risk-free rate only.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which exam question best separates a student who memorised formulas from one who understands TVM?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"State the PV formula.\""], ["B", "\"Define an annuity.\""], ["C", "\"What does PMT stand for?\""], ["D", "\"Two projects pay RM300 a year for 5 years starting now, versus RM300 a year for 5 years starting in one year. Without a calculator, which is worth more at a positive rate, and why? Then explain how the two values relate.\""]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It requires recognising an annuity due versus an ordinary annuity, and reasoning that every payment arrives one period earlier, so the annuity due is worth (1 + r) times as much.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>How does the chapter define an <strong>efficient portfolio</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio with the highest possible return, whatever the risk."], ["B", "The portfolio that provides the highest return for a given level of risk."], ["C", "The portfolio with the largest number of securities."], ["D", "The portfolio with zero standard deviation."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Efficiency is about the trade-off: highest return for a given risk (equivalently, lowest risk for a given return). A ignores risk, C confuses count with quality, and D is only possible in special cases.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is the beta of the market portfolio?</p>", "options": [["A", "1.0"], ["B", "0.5"], ["C", "0"], ["D", "2.0"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta measures sensitivity to the market benchmark, so the market's own beta is 1.0. A beta of 0 means no linear sensitivity to the market.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which risk can be reduced or eliminated through diversification?</p>", "options": [["A", "Unsystematic (diversifiable, firm-specific) risk"], ["B", "Systematic (nondiversifiable) risk"], ["C", "Inflation risk affecting all firms"], ["D", "War and political risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Firm-specific events such as lawsuits and strikes can be diluted by holding many securities. Systematic risk arises from forces that affect similar investments broadly, so diversification cannot remove it.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why is the standard deviation of a portfolio generally <strong>not</strong> the weighted average of its assets' standard deviations?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because standard deviations cannot be averaged by law."], ["B", "Because standard deviation is always larger for portfolios."], ["C", "Because weights do not affect risk."], ["D", "Because portfolio risk also depends on the correlations between the assets: imperfect co-movement lets fluctuations partly offset."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The covariance term 2w<sub>A</sub>w<sub>B</sub>σ<sub>A</sub>σ<sub>B</sub>ρ explains it. Only when ρ = +1 does the portfolio σ equal the weighted average.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement best contrasts beta and standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "They measure exactly the same thing."], ["B", "Both measure only firm-specific risk."], ["C", "Beta measures total volatility; standard deviation measures market sensitivity."], ["D", "Standard deviation measures total volatility; beta measures sensitivity to market movements (systematic risk)."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A stock driven by company-specific news may have a high σ but a low β. Neither replaces the other for every purpose.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does CAPM reward only systematic risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because unsystematic risk does not exist."], ["B", "Because CAPM was designed for bonds only."], ["C", "Because systematic risk is always smaller."], ["D", "Because investors can cheaply diversify away firm-specific risk, so the market has no reason to compensate them for holding it."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>More total risk does not automatically justify a higher CAPM return. What matters is the asset's contribution to market-related risk (an assumption-driven theoretical result).</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Why do index-fund providers emphasise holding the whole market rather than a few stocks, in terms of the chapter's risk decomposition?</p>", "options": [["A", "It eliminates all risk."], ["B", "It removes the need for beta."], ["C", "It guarantees returns above the market."], ["D", "A broad portfolio removes most firm-specific risk at low cost, leaving mainly systematic risk, the part the market compensates."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The diversification curve (Figure 5.8) flattens at the systematic-risk floor. Holding more securities beyond that gives diminishing benefit.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A portfolio is 40% in an asset with E(R) = 10% and 60% in an asset with E(R) = 5%. What is the portfolio's expected return?</p>", "options": [["A", "7.5%"], ["B", "6.0%"], ["C", "7.0%"], ["D", "15.0%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>E(R<sub>p</sub>) = 0.40(10%) + 0.60(5%) = 4% + 3% = <strong>7%</strong>. A is the simple average; D is the sum.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>R<sub>f</sub> = 5%, E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 11% and β = 1.2. What is the CAPM required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "12.2%"], ["B", "18.2%"], ["C", "13.2%"], ["D", "7.2%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>R = 5% + 1.2 x (11% - 5%) = 5% + 7.2% = <strong>12.2%</strong>. B wrongly multiplies beta by the market return; D adds only the premium part.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A portfolio holds 50% in a stock with β = 1.2, 30% with β = 0.8 and 20% with β = 1.5. What is the portfolio beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "1.14"], ["B", "1.00"], ["C", "1.17"], ["D", "1.50"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>β<sub>p</sub> = 0.5(1.2) + 0.3(0.8) + 0.2(1.5) = 0.60 + 0.24 + 0.30 = <strong>1.14</strong>. Portfolio beta is the weighted average of the betas.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%. What does CAPM require from an asset with β = -0.5, and why is that sensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "7%, because all assets need more than the market."], ["B", "4%, because beta never changes required return."], ["C", "-4%, because it must lose money."], ["D", "1%: a negative-beta asset tends to rise when the market falls, so it acts as a hedge and investors accept less return for it."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>4% + (-0.5)(6%) = 1%. Asset value inside a portfolio depends on how it co-moves with everything else, not only on its own expected return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>w<sub>A</sub> = 0.6, w<sub>B</sub> = 0.4, σ<sub>A</sub> = 15%, σ<sub>B</sub> = 25%, ρ = 0.2. What is σ<sub>p</sub>?</p>", "options": [["A", "19.0%"], ["B", "14.7%"], ["C", "12.0%"], ["D", "21.7%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Variance = 0.36(0.0225) + 0.16(0.0625) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(0.15)(0.25)(0.2) = 0.0081 + 0.0100 + 0.0036 = 0.0217; σ<sub>p</sub> = √0.0217 ≈ <strong>14.7%</strong>. A is the weighted average of the σs.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two assets have σ = 20% and 10% and are held 50/50. If they are uncorrelated (ρ = 0), what is the portfolio standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "15.00%"], ["B", "8.66%"], ["C", "11.18%"], ["D", "5.00%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Variance = 0.25(0.04) + 0.25(0.01) = 0.0125; σ<sub>p</sub> = √0.0125 ≈ <strong>11.18%</strong>. A is the weighted average (ρ = +1); B is ρ = -0.5; D is ρ = -1.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A stock has σ = 25%, a correlation of 0.6 with the market, and the market has σ = 15%. What is its beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.40"], ["B", "0.60"], ["C", "1.67"], ["D", "1.00"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>β = ρ(σ<sub>i</sub> / σ<sub>m</sub>) = 0.6 x (25 / 15) = <strong>1.0</strong>. You cannot get beta from the stock's σ alone.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Stock A has β = 0.5 and σ = 45%. Stock B has β = 1.5 and σ = 30%. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is safer on every measure."], ["B", "B has both higher beta and higher total volatility."], ["C", "A has lower market sensitivity but higher total volatility; neither is unconditionally safer."], ["D", "Beta and σ must always rank assets identically."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta captures systematic exposure; σ captures total variation including firm-specific risk. A low beta does not make a stock low-volatility overall.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A security has E(R) = 9%, β = 0.7, R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%. What is its expected alpha?</p>", "options": [["A", "-0.8%"], ["B", "0%"], ["C", "+5.0%"], ["D", "+0.8%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Required = 4% + 0.7(6%) = 8.2%. Alpha = 9% - 8.2% = <strong>+0.8%</strong>: above the SML.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>During a global crisis the correlation between two assets (σ = 20% and 10%, 50/50) rises from 0 to 0.8. Approximately what happens to σ<sub>p</sub>?</p>", "options": [["A", "It falls from 11.2% to 8%."], ["B", "It becomes 5%."], ["C", "It stays at 11.2%."], ["D", "It rises from about 11.2% to about 14.3%."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Variance = 0.0125 + 2(0.25)(0.2)(0.1)ρ = 0.0125 + 0.01ρ. At ρ = 0.8: 0.0205, so σ<sub>p</sub> ≈ 14.3%. Historical low correlation is not guaranteed future low correlation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why can estimated betas be unstable?</p>", "options": [["A", "They are estimated from historical returns over a particular window and benchmark, so changes in the firm, the market or the estimation period alter the estimate."], ["B", "Betas are set by regulators each quarter."], ["C", "Beta is always exactly 1."], ["D", "Beta depends only on dividends."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta describes a linear relationship estimated from past data; it is not a mechanical multiplier. This limits how confidently SML-based conclusions can be drawn.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>A stock's firm-specific risk doubles, but its covariance with the market is unchanged. What does CAPM predict for its required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "It doubles."], ["B", "It is unchanged: beta depends on covariance with the market, and unsystematic risk is not priced."], ["C", "It falls to the risk-free rate."], ["D", "It becomes negative."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>β = Cov(R<sub>i</sub>, R<sub>m</sub>) / Var(R<sub>m</sub>). Unchanged covariance means unchanged beta, so the same required return. Investors could diversify the extra risk away.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A stock with β = 1.5 is expected by an analyst to return 15%. R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%. Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "It is overvalued: below the SML."], ["B", "Required return is 13%, so expected alpha is +2%: above the SML, potentially undervalued, but only as reliable as the forecast."], ["C", "It is fairly priced exactly on the SML."], ["D", "CAPM cannot be applied with β above 1."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Required = 4% + 1.5(6%) = 13%. Positive alpha is not proof of a bargain; it may reflect an inaccurate forecast or an inadequate model.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Adding more stocks to a portfolio always reduces risk.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incorrect: a highly correlated additional stock adds little diversification; what matters is whether the new asset reduces covariance or concentration."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct, but only for stocks above beta 1."], ["D", "Incorrect, because adding stocks always increases risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Number of assets ≠ quality of diversification. Forty bank stocks are still one big bet on banking.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"I'll choose a portfolio below the efficient frontier because it feels safer.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Sensible: feelings determine efficiency."], ["B", "Flawed: a dominated portfolio offers the same risk for less return; a cautious investor should pick a lower-risk point on the frontier instead."], ["C", "Correct, because the frontier is always riskier."], ["D", "Correct, because portfolios below the frontier have zero risk."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Comfort and efficiency are different things. Risk preference selects among efficient portfolios; it is not a reason to hold an inefficient one.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>Portfolios A, B and C have expected alphas of +0.8%, +1.4% and +1.0%, and standard deviations of 12%, 18% and 28%. Evaluate \"Portfolio B must be chosen because it has the highest alpha.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incomplete: the choice also depends on risk tolerance, confidence in the forecasts, feasibility and total and systematic risk."], ["C", "Incorrect, because alpha is meaningless."], ["D", "Correct, because alpha equals return."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Alpha is one input. A cautious investor may prefer A (lowest σ and beta) despite a smaller alpha.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Portfolio W (β = 0.91) is less market-sensitive than V (β = 1.195). Evaluate: \"W therefore has lower total standard deviation.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Correct, because beta equals σ."], ["C", "Not implied: beta captures only market-related sensitivity; W could still have higher total volatility from firm-specific risk or concentration."], ["D", "Incorrect, because W has a higher beta."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta alone cannot reveal total σ or expected return. The chapter stresses that the two measure different dimensions.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An optimiser built from historical estimates recommends a very concentrated \"efficient\" portfolio. What is the best critical response?</p>", "options": [["A", "Accept it; mathematics is always right."], ["B", "Recognise estimation error: small errors in expected returns and correlations can swing optimal weights, so test robustness, add constraints and compare with simple diversified alternatives."], ["C", "Reject MPT entirely."], ["D", "Invest only in the highest-return asset."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter warns that MPT depends on statistical estimates, so a mathematically optimal portfolio can perform poorly in reality if the estimates are poor.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>A manager reports positive alpha against CAPM for ten years. Which interpretation is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Consistent with skill, but also with an inadequate pricing model, omitted risk factors, mismeasured beta or luck; further tests are needed."], ["B", "Proof of superior skill."], ["C", "Proof that markets are inefficient."], ["D", "Meaningless because alpha is always zero."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes caution that positive alpha could reflect inaccurate expected-return estimates or an inadequate pricing model. It is a joint test of the manager and the model.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Asset A has σ = 30% and asset B has σ = 10%, with ρ = -1. Which weights make the portfolio riskless?</p>", "options": [["A", "50% A, 50% B"], ["B", "75% A, 25% B"], ["C", "25% A, 75% B"], ["D", "10% A, 90% B"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Set w<sub>A</sub>σ<sub>A</sub> = w<sub>B</sub>σ<sub>B</sub>: w<sub>A</sub> x 30 = (1 - w<sub>A</sub>) x 10, so w<sub>A</sub> = 10 / 40 = <strong>25%</strong>. Check: 0.25 x 30 = 7.5 = 0.75 x 10.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Stock X has β = 1.4 and Stock Y has β = 0.6. What weight in X gives a two-stock portfolio with β = 1.0?</p>", "options": [["A", "25%"], ["B", "60%"], ["C", "50%"], ["D", "75%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>w(1.4) + (1 - w)(0.6) = 1.0 → 0.8w = 0.4 → <strong>w = 0.5</strong>. Beta is linear in the weights.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which sequence best constructs a portfolio for an investor, per the chapter's reconciliation of traditional and modern approaches?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy the ten highest-return stocks."], ["B", "Copy the most popular fund."], ["C", "Decide how much risk you can bear, diversify across securities and industries, check correlations, use portfolio beta to hold risk at an acceptable level, and evaluate alternatives for the best return at that risk."], ["D", "Hold only familiar domestic companies for comfort."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This reproduces the chapter's policy: traditional diversification and judgment plus MPT's statistical discipline (correlations and beta).</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which exam question best separates a student who memorised the SML from one who understands what it says?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Two stocks each expect 11%. Stock P has β = 0.5 and Stock Q has β = 1.5; with R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%, decide for each whether it is above, on or below the SML, then explain what could make your conclusion wrong.\""], ["B", "\"State the CAPM equation.\""], ["C", "\"What does SML stand for?\""], ["D", "\"Draw the SML from memory.\""]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>P requires 7% (alpha +4%, above) and Q requires 13% (alpha -2%, below). The \"what could be wrong\" part requires evaluating forecasts, beta estimates and the model's assumptions.</p>"}];
